var corpus_default = /* @__PURE__ */ JSON.parse("[{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":1,\"text\":\"DRINKING WATER QUALITY ASSURANCE RULES 2022 Released 25 July 2022 Revised 29 November 2024\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=1\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":2,\"text\":\"Ko wai, Ko au, Ko tātou Ko te wai ahau, ko ahau te wai. He whakaaturanga tātou nō te wai. Ko te ora te wai ko te ora o te tangata. He taonga te wai me tiaki. Ko wai tātou. Ko wai tātou. I am wai, wai is me. We are reflections of our wai. The health of te wai is the health of te tangata. Wai is a taonga that must be protected. Ko wai tātou. We are wai. Wai is us. This document is a consolidated version of the Drinking Water Quality Assurance Rules 2022 including amendments made by the Drinking Water Quality Assurance (Very Small to Medium Drinking Water Supplies) Amendment Rules 2024.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=2\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":3,\"text\":\"Contents page List of tables ............................................................................................................... 5 1. Introduction .............................................................................................. 6 1.1 Rule objectives .................................................................................................. 6 1.2 Effective date of the Rules ................................................................................ 6 1.3 General guidance .............................................................................................. 6 1.4 Components of a drinking water supply .......................................................... 7 1.5 Categories of drinking water supply .................................................................\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=3\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":3,\"text\":\"7 1.6 Structure of the Rules ....................................................................................... 9 2. Drinking water supply categories and Rules modules ............................... 10 3. Application of the Rules modules............................................................. 11 3.1 Compliance and reporting .............................................................................. 11 4. Compliance Rules Modules ...................................................................... 14 4.1 G General Rules Module ................................................................................. 14 4.2 VSC Very Small Communities Rules Module .................................................. 20 4.3 S1 Source Water Rules Module ......................................................................\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=3\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":3,\"text\":\"21 4.4 T1 Treatment Rules Module ........................................................................... 23 4.5 D1 Distribution System Rules Module ............................................................ 25 4.6 S2 Source Water Rules Module ...................................................................... 26 4.7 T2 Treatment Rules Module ........................................................................... 28 4.8 D2 Distribution System Rules Module ............................................................ 32 4.9 S3 Source Water Rules Module ...................................................................... 34 4.9.1 S3 Source Water Protozoa Log Credit Treatment Requirements.......... 34 4.9.2 S3 Sanitary Bore Head Requirements....................................................\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=3\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":3,\"text\":\"35 4.9.3 S3 Source Water Monitoring Rules........................................................ 36 4.10 T3 Treatment Rules Module............................................................................. 39 4.10.1 T3 Bacterial Rules................................................................................. 39 4.10.2 T3 Protozoal Rules ............................................................................... 45 4.10.3 T3 Chemical Rules ................................................................................ 62 4.10.4 T3 Cyanotoxin Rules............................................................................. 65 4.11 D3 Distribution System Rules Module ............................................................. 66 4.11.1 D3 Backflow Protection Rules..............................................................\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=3\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":3,\"text\":\"66 4.11.2 D3 New and Repaired Watermains Hygiene Procedures Rules .......... 67 4.11.3 D3 Facilities Operation, Maintenance and Disinfection Rules ............ 68 4.11.4 D3 Residual Disinfection, Disinfection By-product, and Plumbosolvent Metal Rules ..................................................................................................... 69 4.11.5 D3 Microbiological Monitoring Rules .................................................. 73\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=3\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":4,\"text\":\"4.12 WC Water Carrier Service Rules Module ............................................ 75 4.13 TDWS Temporary Drinking Water Supplies Rules (Revoked) ............. 77 4.14 VP Rules for Supplies with Varying Population Module ..................... 78 5. Definitions............................................................................................... 80\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=4\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":5,\"text\":\"List of tables Table 1. Rules Modules ........................................................................................................................................... 9 Table 2. Categories of drinking water supply and Rules that apply to them. ....................................................... 10 Table 3. Removed .................................................................................................................................................. 17 Table 4. Removed .................................................................................................................................................. 17 Table 5. Bacterial reporting requirements for level 3 Rules. ................................................................................. 18 Table 6.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=5\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":5,\"text\":\"Protozoal reporting requirements for level 3 Rules. ................................................................................ 18 Table 7. Distribution zone reporting requirements for level 3 Rules. .................................................................... 19 Table 8. Removed .................................................................................................................................................. 22 Table 9. Removed .................................................................................................................................................. 22 Table 10. Removed ................................................................................................................................................ 24 Table 11.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=5\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":5,\"text\":\"Removed ................................................................................................................................................ 25 Table 12. Removed ................................................................................................................................................ 27 Table 13. Removed ................................................................................................................................................ 27 Table 14. Removed ................................................................................................................................................ 31 Table 15. Removed ................................................................................................................................................ 33 Table 16.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=5\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":5,\"text\":\"S3 Source Water Monitoring Determinands.......................................................................................... 37 Table 17. S3 Class 1, Class 2 and Class 3 Groundwater Source Monitoring Determinands................................... 37 Table 18. S3 Raw Water Monitoring Parameters ................................................................................................. 38 Table 19. T3 Requirements for Drinking Water Disinfected with Chlorine ............................................................ 39 Table 20. T3 Requirements for Drinking Water Disinfected with Chlorine Dioxide ............................................... 41 Table 21. T3 Requirements for Water Disinfected with Ozone ............................................................................. 42 Table 22.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=5\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":5,\"text\":\"T3 Requirements for UV Disinfection..................................................................................................... 44 Table 23. T3 Requirements for Coagulation, Flocculation and Sedimentation without Filtration ........................ 46 Table 24. T3 Requirements for Coagulation, Flocculation and Direct Filtration ................................................... 48 Table 25. T3 Requirements for Coagulation, Flocculation, Sedimentation and Filtration..................................... 50 Table 26. T3 Requirements for Second Stage Filtration ........................................................................................ 51 Table 27. T3 Requirements for Slow Sand Filtration ............................................................................................. 52 Table 28.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=5\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":5,\"text\":\"T3 Requirements for Cartridge Filtration............................................................................................... 54 Table 29. T3 Requirements for Membrane Filtration ............................................................................................ 56 Table 30. T3 C.t values (min.mg/L) for Cryptosporidium inactivation by ozone ................................................... 57 Table 31. T3 Requirements for Ozone Disinfection ............................................................................................... 58 Table 32. T3 Requirements for UV Disinfection..................................................................................................... 60 Table 33. T3 Treatment Chemical Determinand Minimum Sampling Frequencies ............................................... 64 Table 34.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=5\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":5,\"text\":\"T3 Possible Treatment Chemical Determinand Monitoring................................................................... 64 Table 35. D3 FAC sampling frequency ................................................................................................................... 71 Table 36. D3 Number of continuous monitoring analysers required in each zone................................................ 71 Table 37. D3 Disinfection by-product sampling frequency .................................................................................... 72 Table 38. D3 Distribution zone for plumbosolvent metals .................................................................................... 72 Table 39. D3 Minimum Microbiological Sampling Frequencies ............................................................................ 74 Table 40.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=5\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":5,\"text\":\"Removed ................................................................................................................................................ 77\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=5\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":6,\"text\":\"1. Introduction The Drinking Water Quality Assurance Rules (“Rules”) have been prepared by Taumata Arowai in accordance with section 49 of the Water Services Act 2021, including the public consultation requirements set out in section 53 of the Act. The Rules are secondary legislation and are disallowable by the House of Representatives under the Legislation Act 2019. 1.1 Rule objectives These Rules primarily impose requirements relating to drinking water supplier duties to: 1. supply safe drinking water 1 2. ensure that drinking water complies with the Water Services (Drinking Water Standards for New Zealand) Regulations 2022. 2 Aspects of the Rules also relate to drinking water supplier functions or duties, under other provisions in Part 2 of the Act.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=6\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":6,\"text\":\"1.2 Effective date of the Rules The Rules came into force on 14 November 2022 and the amendments in this revised version are in force from 1 January 2025. 1.3 General guidance This section provides some general guidance about the Rules. The Rules set out minimum compliance requirements and drinking water suppliers may use or undertake additional measures, including the management of risks specifically identified in relation to their supplies, to ensure that water provided to consumers is safe to drink. Definitions of technical terms and abbreviations used in the Rules are set out in the Definitions section (Section 5). Unless otherwise defined in Section 5, terms defined in the Act that are used in the Rules have the same meaning as in the Act.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=6\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":6,\"text\":\"These Rules cover water quality operational requirements from source water abstraction to the point of supply to a consumer, typically at the boundary toby. In most situations, the Building Act 2004 regulates water supplies beyond this point. Compliance with the Rules is intended to ensure that water provided by drinking water suppliers does not exceed the MAVs for key determinands, as set out in the Drinking Water Standards. 1 Water Services Act 2021, section 21. 2 Water Services Act 2021, section 22.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=6\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":7,\"text\":\"Where MAVs cannot be (or are not) used to measure compliance, treatment efﬁcacy is used as the surrogate criteria for demonstrating compliance. When surrogate criteria are used, the Rules specify operational requirements. Compliance with these provides conﬁdence that a treatment barrier is working effectively. FAC and compliance with ﬁlter performance parameters such as turbidity are examples of surrogate criteria. These surrogate criteria have the important benefit of being able to be measured frequently, and even continuously, on-site so that near-real time feedback about treatment performance is available. The Rules do not set quality requirements for bottled water or water used solely for industrial or agricultural purposes as these are not covered by the Act.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=7\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":7,\"text\":\"For people with certain medical conditions, or for uses of water for purposes other than drinking (e.g., kidney dialysis), additional or other water quality criteria may apply. 1.4 Components of a drinking water supply A drinking water supply comprises the infrastructure and processes used to abstract water from a source, and to store, treat, transmit, or transport drinking water to a point of supply for consumers or other drinking water suppliers. It will generally have one or more of each of the following components: 1. source water abstraction 2. water treatment plant 3. distribution system. Through the registration process, Taumata Arowai allocates unique identifier numbers to registered water supplies, sources, treatment plants, and distribution zones.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=7\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":7,\"text\":\"1.5 Categories of drinking water supply The Rules have been prepared for the following drinking water supply categories: 1. Very Small Communities Drinking water supplies of any configuration or arrangement (excluding domestic self-supplies) that provide drinking water to a population of up to 25 people. In circumstances when the population supplied fluctuates, the population supplied may increase to up to 50 people for up to 60 days in any 12 month period. 3 2. Networked Supplies Drinking water supplies that provide drinking water via a distribution system at a pressure and volume to meet consumer demand, or at a restricted flow and volume. These supplies may include storage facilities within the network to buffer demand. 3 Removed\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=7\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":8,\"text\":\"The Rules have been prepared for Networked Supplies with the following population sizes: a) Small 26 – 100 people. Varying Population module requirements can apply to this drinking water supply category. b) Medium 101 – 500 people. Varying Population module requirements can apply to this drinking water supply category. c) Large >500 people. 3. Self-supplied Buildings Self-supplied Buildings are water supplies (excluding domestic self-supplies) which provide drinking water to up to ten buildings on one site (within the boundaries of one property, or within the boundaries of two or more properties with common ownership arrangements) and provide water to more than 25 people. The Rules have been prepared for Self-supplied Buildings with the following population sizes: (a) Small 26 – 100 people.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=8\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":8,\"text\":\"Varying Population module requirements can apply to this drinking water supply category. (b) Medium 101 – 500 people. Varying Population module requirements can apply to this drinking water supply category. (c) Large >500 people. 4. Water Carrier Services Drinking water supplies that involve the transport of drinking water in a vehicle or vessel with a water tank (e.g., a truck, trailer, or rail wagon) and supply to consumers or other drinking water suppliers, often to a storage tank on a property. Typically, Water Carrier Services provide drinking water to houses that have their own supplies but need the quantity of stored water to be augmented. Water Carrier Services can also augment other drinking water supplies, particularly during droughts and emergencies and provide water to planned events.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=8\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":8,\"text\":\"Water Carrier Services that fill tankers from a supply that provides water specifically for Water Carrier Services, must ensure that supply is separately registered and complies with the Water Carrier Supplies Rules. 5. Water Carrier Supplies Water Carrier Supplies are drinking water supplies that provide water specifically to fill tanker vehicles that are owned or operated by a Water Carrier Service. 6. Community Drinking Water Stations Community Drinking Water Stations are drinking water supplies that provide drinking water from a single point of supply to a community who collect the water in containers. Public taps or container filling stations that are connected to a Networked Supply (e.g., community taps that provide water which is chlorinated and then de- chlorinated) are not considered to be Community Drinking Water Stations.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=8\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":9,\"text\":\"1.6 Structure of the Rules The Rules are structured as ‘modules’, with the main modules covering general rules, source water, treatment systems and distribution systems. Additional modules are provided for some specific drinking water supply categories or situations. The modules and module codes are: 1. General Rules Module (G) – including rules that outline the monitoring and assurance rules that water suppliers need to report their compliance against to Taumata Arowai and the frequency of that reporting, to Taumata Arowai, sample collection and calibration of analytical instruments. 2. Very Small Communities Rules Module (VSC) – includes microbiological monitoring requirements and additional requirements for when population limits are exceeded. 3.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=9\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":9,\"text\":\"Source Water Rules Modules (S1, S2, S3) – three complexity levels dependant on the size of the supply, covering rules for source water sampling. 4. Treatment Rules Modules (T1, T2, T3) – three complexity levels dependant on the size of the supply, covering rules for bacterial compliance, protozoal compliance, chemical compliance and cyanotoxin compliance. 5. Distribution System Rules Modules (D1, D2, D3) – three complexity levels dependant on the size of the supply, covering rules for backflow prevention, preventing recontamination of stored water, hygiene practices for maintenance and upgrades, monitoring FAC, disinfection by-products and microbial water quality. 6. Water Carrier Services Rules Module (WC) – including Rules which cover the transport of drinking water. 7.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=9\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":9,\"text\":\"Varying Population Rules Module (VP) – covers additional monitoring requirements that must be met when the usual base population of a drinking water supply increases for limited periods of time. The following table sets out the Rules modules and complexity levels. Subsequent sections set out which modules each supply category must demonstrate compliance against. Table 1. Rules Modules G VSC S1 S2 S3 T1 T2 T3 D1 D2 D3 WC VP\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=9\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":10,\"text\":\"2. Drinking water supply categories and Rules modules Table 2 below outlines the drinking water supply categories, and the Rules modules that drinking water suppliers must demonstrate compliance against. Table 2. Categories of drinking water supply and rules that apply to them. Categories of drinking water supplies Rules modules that compliance is demonstrated against 1. Very Small Communities 0 – 25 people and 26 – 50 people for up to VSC 60 days in any 12-month period (includes flexibility for exceeding population limits) 2. Networked Supplies Small (26 – 100 people) G + S1 + T1 + D1 + VP Medium (101 – 500 people) G + S2 + T2 + D2 + VP Large (>500 people) G + S3 + T3 + D3 3. Self-supplied Buildings Small (26 – 100 people) G + S1 + T1 + VP Medium (101 – 500 people) G + S2 + T2 + VP4 Large (>500 people) G + S3 + T3 5 4.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=10\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":10,\"text\":\"Water Carrier Services Any population size G + WC 5. Water Carrier Supplies Any population size G + S1 + T1 6. Community Drinking Water Stations Any population size G + S1 + T1 4 Removed 5 Where more than one building is supplied, chlorine must be used to demonstrate bacterial compliance.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=10\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":11,\"text\":\"3. Application of the Rules modules Drinking water suppliers must select the Rules modules that apply to each of their registered supplies based on the category that best fits their drinking water supply. Drinking water suppliers must demonstrate compliance against those Rules modules for each registered supply. For example, if a Networked Supply serves 10,000 people, level 3 rules must be used for all source, treatment, and distribution zone compliance, regardless of the number of people served by any single source, treatment plant, or distribution zone. If a distribution zone in a community of 10,000 people serves only 450 people, that zone must still demonstrate compliance against the level 3 rules.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=11\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":11,\"text\":\"Where population thresholds are provided, the population supplied should be calculated on the base population, the population that is normally supplied drinking water regardless of any seasonal increases. Any drinking water supplier can elect to demonstrate compliance with a higher level of source, treatment, or distribution zone Rules module if they choose. For example, a drinking water supply which is required to comply with the level 2 Rules modules for source, treatment, and distribution zone, may choose to demonstrate compliance against the level 2 Rules modules for source and distribution zone, but the level 3 Rules module for treatment if that is more suitable for them. In that case, the Rules modules that they would demonstrate compliance against would be G + S2 + T3 + D2 + VP.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=11\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":11,\"text\":\"If a drinking water supplier chooses to comply with a higher-level Rules module e.g., T3, they must comply with all of the rules in that module. It is up to each water supplier to determine whether they will demonstrate compliance against a higher-level Rules module, and over which compliance periods. 3.1 Compliance and reporting Drinking water suppliers only need to demonstrate compliance against the Rules for periods when a drinking water supply is operating. Example 1: A bore may only be used during the summer period when there is a high level of demand. The source water requirements for that bore would only need to be demonstrated for the period that the bore is in operation. Example 2: A treatment plant may be off-line for several reasons, compliance does not need to be demonstrated for the period that the plant is not operating.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=11\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":11,\"text\":\"However, if a treatment plant operates for only part of a compliance period, data/information must be reported for that compliance period. Example 3: A distribution zone is expected to be in operation continually, even when a treatment plant is not, so demonstration of compliance for the distribution zone will be continual. Drinking water suppliers must assess their compliance with the Rules and provide a report of compliance to Taumata Arowai.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=11\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":12,\"text\":\"Reporting requirements Drinking water suppliers submit compliance reports to Taumata Arowai at varying frequencies depending on the level of rules they follow. Compliance reports are reported in an approved form which Taumata Arowai makes available to suppliers. Reporting requirements are set out in the General Rules: G1, G2.1, G2.2, G3, G4, and G5. Each compliance report must indicate the number of compliance periods in the reporting period that the supply was non-compliant with and, where applicable, outline the reason for any non-compliance. Compliance periods are the length of time over which compliance is assessed for reporting purposes. Two types of rules are assigned a compliance period, monitoring rules and assurance rules. Some rules don’t have a compliance period, these are non- reporting rules.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=12\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":12,\"text\":\"Monitoring rules Monitoring rules set out requirements to monitor the quality of source water and treated water. They cover determinands and parameters that need to be either continuously monitored, or regularly sampled. Monitoring rules have compliance periods of 1 day, 1 month, 3 months, or 1 year, which typically depends on the frequency of monitoring required in the rule. 1-day compliance periods A monitoring rule which requires a determinand or parameter to be continuously monitored or monitored daily, irrespective of the period of the day that the supply is operating, has a compliance period of 1 day, i.e. 24 hours (midnight to midnight). 1-month compliance periods A monitoring rule which requires a determinand or parameter to be monitored on a monthly or weekly basis, e.g.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=12\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":12,\"text\":\"2 per week, 8 per month, etc., has a compliance period of 1 month. 3-month compliance periods A monitoring rule which requires a determinand or parameter to be monitored at least every 3 months has a compliance period of 3 months. 1-year compliance periods All other monitoring rules have a compliance period of 1 year. Note: Where monitoring occurs at least every 3 years or at least every 5 years, the rule may be reported as compliant if the sampling frequency has been met by the end of the calendar year being reported on. Assurance rules Assurance rules cover activities that water suppliers need to undertake, for example the preparation of a backflow prevention programme or a distribution zone sampling plan.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=12\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":12,\"text\":\"Assurance rules are not used to demonstrate compliance with the Drinking Water Standards but indicate whether water suppliers undertake activities that contribute to the provision of safe drinking water. Assurance rules have a compliance period of 1 year.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=12\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":13,\"text\":\"Non-Reporting rules Some rules are designated as non-reporting rules and water suppliers are not required to report on their performance against those rules, though they are still expected to comply with the requirements of the rules. Non-reporting rules have no compliance period as they do not have to be reported on.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=13\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":14,\"text\":\"4. Compliance Rules Modules 4.1 G General Rules Module Rule Requirement Rule type Compliance number period G1. Assurance 1 Year Drinking water suppliers following level 1 Rules modules must report annually— (a) whether they complied with each monitoring rule requirement; and (b) the number of quarters for which each monitoring rule requirement was not complied with during each calendar year; and (c) the supply component ID, sample ID, sample date, and test results for all samples analysed by a laboratory during the year; and (d) within 40 working days of the end of each calendar year; and (e) in an approved form. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 G2.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=14\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":14,\"text\":\"Revoked Rule revoked on 29 November 2024 G2.1 Assurance 1 Year Drinking water suppliers following level 2 monitoring rules must report quarterly— (a) whether they complied with each monitoring rule requirement; and (b) the number of months for which each monitoring rule requirement was not complied with during each quarter; and (c) the supply component ID, sample ID, sample date, and test results for all samples analysed by a laboratory taken during the quarter; and (d) within 20 working days of the end of each quarter; and (e) in an approved form. Rule inserted on 29 November 2024\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=14\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":15,\"text\":\"Rule Requirement Rule type Compliance number period G2.2 Drinking water suppliers following level 2 assurance rules Assurance 1 Year must report annually— (a) whether they complied with each assurance rule during each calendar year; and (b) within 40 working days of the end of each calendar year; and (c) in an approved form. Rule inserted on 29 November 2024 G3. Drinking water suppliers demonstrating compliance Assurance As set out in against level 3 Rules must report to Taumata Arowai the tables 5, 6 water quality monitoring information set out in tables 5, and 7 6 and 7. G4. Drinking water suppliers reporting against level 3 Assurance As set out in monitoring rules that are not included in tables 5, 6, and the relevant 7 must report annually within 40 working days of the end rules of each calendar year.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=15\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":15,\"text\":\"Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 G5. Drinking water suppliers demonstrating compliance with Assurance 1 Year level 3 rules, must report on their performance against all assurance rules annually within 40 days of the end of each calendar year. 6 Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 G6. All samples collected from drinking water supplies for Assurance 1 Year monitoring that are analysed by laboratories, must be labelled with the unique source, treatment plant, distribution zone, or Water Carrier Service identifier allocated by Taumata Arowai, to show where the sample was collected from and the time and date that the sample was collected. G7.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=15\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":15,\"text\":\"Drinking water suppliers must take reasonably Assurance 1 year practicable steps to ensure that samples for E. coli, total coliforms, or other microbiological contaminants are delivered to a laboratory within 24 hours of the sample being collected, and at a water temperature that is no higher than the water temperature at the time of sampling but above zero degrees Celsius. 6 Removed\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=15\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":16,\"text\":\"Rule Requirement Rule type Compliance number period G8. All water samples that require laboratory analysis and Assurance 1 year are used to demonstrate compliance with these Rules must be: a. analysed by a laboratory accredited by IANZ for the type of analysis being undertaken; and b. collected according to any instructions and specifications provided by the laboratory. G9. Equipment used for the analysis of single samples (grab Assurance 1 Year samples) by drinking water suppliers, to demonstrate compliance with any rule, must be calibrated/verified in accordance with the instrument manufacturer’s specified procedures and frequency. G10. All work (planned or unplanned) on a water supply must Assurance 1 Year be completed by suitably trained or experienced personnel. G11.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=16\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":16,\"text\":\"Drinking water suppliers must prepare a hygiene code of Assurance 1 Year practice for people working on a water supply which must include: a. maintenance of personal hygiene at all times; and b. prohibition of people working on a water system who are experiencing any gastrointestinal illness; and c. protection of the work site, materials, and tools from contamination; and d. how all reasonable steps will be taken to minimise the entry of contamination into the water supply during any activity. G12. Continuous on-line monitoring equipment used to Assurance 1 Year demonstrate compliance with any rule must: a. be calibrated in accordance with the instrument manufacturer’s specified procedures and frequency; and b. have calibration verified in accordance with the instrument manufacturer’s specified procedures.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=16\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":17,\"text\":\"Rule Requirement Rule type Compliance number period G13. For continuous monitoring equipment that is used to Assurance 1 Year demonstrate compliance against treatment Rules (T1, T2, T3), the separation between data records must be no more than 1 minute. 7 G14. Generation of continuous monitoring data that is used to Assurance 1 Year demonstrate compliance against T3 treatment Rules or D3 Distribution Rules, must not be interrupted for a period of more than 15 consecutive minutes, or for a total of more than 72 minutes in any one-day compliance period, for compliance to be achieved. G15. For continuous monitoring equipment that is used to Assurance 1 Year assess source water or to demonstrate compliance against distribution zone Rules, the separation between data records must be no more than 30 minutes. G16.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=17\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":17,\"text\":\"Continuous monitoring equipment used to monitor FAC Assurance 1 Year in distribution zones must be appropriately pH and temperature compensated. G17. Where continuous monitoring equipment that is used to Monitoring Dependent on demonstrate compliance (excludes source water the parameter monitoring) fails, or is not otherwise able to provide and data, grab samples can be taken to substitute for circumstances continuous data if analyses of the parameters is undertaken for at least every 30-minute period that the continuous monitoring equipment is not operating. Table 3. Removed Table 4. Removed 8 9 7 Separation between data records of up to five minutes is allowed for FAC analysers and fluoride analysers where the minimum cycle time specified by the analyser manufacturer exceeds 1 minute. 8 Removed 9 Removed\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=17\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":18,\"text\":\"Table 5. Bacterial reporting requirements for level 3 Rules. Rule 10 Parameter Compliance Period Reporting period 11 If chlorine is the primary disinfectant T3.2 Chlorine C.t 1 Day 1 Month If chlorine dioxide is the primary disinfectant T3.8 Chlorine dioxide C.t 1 Day 1 Month If ozone is the primary disinfectant T3.13 Ozone residual 1 Day 1 Month If UV is the primary disinfectant T3.17 UV dose 1 Day 1 Month Table 6. Protozoal reporting requirements for level 3 Rules.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=18\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":18,\"text\":\"Reporting Rule 12 Parameter Compliance Period period 13 For Coagulation, Flocculation and Sedimentation without Filtration T3.24 Turbidity 1 Day 1 Month For Coagulation, Flocculation and Sedimentation with Filtration, Second Stage Filtration, Slow Sand Filtration, Membrane Filtration or Cartridge Filtration Depending on process Turbidity 1 Day 1 Month used, either: (T3.27 and T3.28) (T3.31 and T3.32) (T3.35 and T3.36) (T3.39 and T3.40) (T3.43 and T3.44) (T3.47 and T3.48) (T3.51 and T3.52) (T3.60 and T3.61) (T3.65 and T3.66) (T3.76 and T3.77) For ozone T3.81 Ozone C.t 1 Day 1 Month If UV is the primary disinfectant T3.86 UV dose 1 Day 1 Month 10 Reporting is only required against one of the rules in this column depending on whether chlorine, ozone or UV is used as the primary disinfectant.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=18\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":18,\"text\":\"11 Report must be provided to Taumata Arowai within 10 working days of the end of each month. 12 Reporting is only required against one pair (in brackets) of the rules in this column depending on the process that is used to demonstrate an effective protozoa barrier. 13 Report must be provided to Taumata Arowai within 10 working days of the end of each month.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=18\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":19,\"text\":\"Table 7. Distribution zone reporting requirements for level 3 Rules. Rule Number Parameter Compliance period Reporting period 14 D3.19 FAC 1 Month 1 Month D3.29 E. coli, total coliforms 1 Month 1 Month 14 Report must be provided to Taumata Arowai within 10 working days of the end of each month.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=19\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":20,\"text\":\"4.2 VSC Very Small Communities Rules Module Rule Requirement Rule type Compliance number period VSC.1 Drinking water must be monitored at least every 6 Non-reporting N/A months for the following: (a) E. coli: (b) total coliforms. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 VSC.2 All water samples taken for VSC.1 must be— Non-reporting N/A (a) analysed by a laboratory accredited by IANZ for those tests; and (b) collected according to the requirements of that laboratory. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 VSC.3 When the population exceeds 25 people for more than Non-reporting N/A 60 days in any 12-month period, the relevant Rules modules for Small Supplies set out in Table 2 must be followed, for the remainder of the calendar year.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=20\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":20,\"text\":\"Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 VSC.4 When the population exceeds 50 people for a planned Non-reporting N/A event, the following requirements apply: (a) during the event, water must be— (i) ﬁltered; and either (ii) disinfected with UV light; or (iii) chlorinated: (b) drinking water must be monitored in the week before the event and weekly if the event exceeds 1 week for the following: (i) E. coli: (ii) total coliforms. Rule inserted on 29 November 2024\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=20\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":21,\"text\":\"4.3 S1 Source Water Rules Module Rule Requirement Rule type Compliance number period S1.1 Surface and groundwater sources must be monitored— Monitoring 3 Months (a) at least every 3 months for the following: (i) E. coli: (ii) total coliforms; and (b) at least every 3 years for the following: Monitoring 1 Year (i) arsenic: (ii) boron: (iii) nitrate: (iv) manganese. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 S1.2 Roof water sources must be monitored— Monitoring 3 Months (a) at least every 3 months for the following: (i) E. coli: (ii) total coliforms; and (b) at least every 3 years for the following: Monitoring 1 Year (i) cadmium: (ii) copper: (iii) lead.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=21\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":21,\"text\":\"Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 S1.3 Any chemical determinands that are identified as Monitoring 1 Year presenting a risk to the supply or are found to exceed 50% of their MAV in source water samples must be monitored at least annually until 3 consecutive results from source water samples are less than the 50% of the MAV. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 S1.4 Samples collected under— Non-reporting N/A (a) rule S1.1 must be collected at the abstraction point or treatment plant prior to treatment and/or mixing with other sources; and (b) rule S1.2 must be collected at the raw water storage tank outlet and prior to any treatment. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=21\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":22,\"text\":\"S1.5 The following measures must be taken in relation to Non-reporting N/A surface water intakes: (a) each month between October and May (inclusive)— (i) the water and area around and upstream of the intake must be inspected for the presence of benthic cyanobacteria mats and planktonic cyanobacterial growth; or (ii) water must be monitored for cyanobacterial cell count at the treatment plant prior to mixing and treatment: (b) if there is evidence of cyanobacterial growth, steps must be taken to evaluate the cyanotoxin risk to consumers: (c) if there is a risk of supplying water with cyanotoxins that exceed MAVs, abstraction of water must be discontinued, an alternative source used, or treatment installed, until the risk is no longer present.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=22\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":22,\"text\":\"Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 S1.6 Consumer taste or odour complaints, which have the Non-reporting N/A potential to relate to cyanobacteria, must be— (a) recorded; and (b) investigated to determine the cause. Rule inserted on 29 November 2024 Table 8. Removed 15 Table 9. Removed 15 Removed\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=22\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":23,\"text\":\"4.4 T1 Treatment Rules Module Rule Requirement Rule type Compliance number period T1.1 Water leaving a treatment plant must be monitored at Monitoring 3 months least every 3 months for the following: (a) E. coli: (b) total coliforms: (c) turbidity. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 T1.2 Any additional determinand(s) which are identified as Monitoring 1 Year presenting a risk to the supply must be monitored at least every 3 months in water leaving a treatment plant until 3 consecutive results from treated water samples confirm the determinand(s) to be less than 50% of the MAV.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=23\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":23,\"text\":\"Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 T1.3 All water, excluding groundwater abstracted from a Non-reporting N/A depth of greater than 30 metres, must be filtered by a— (a) cartridge filter system that includes a 5 micron (nominal) or smaller pore size; or (b) back-washable media filter; or (c) slow sand (biological) filter; or (d) membrane filter. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 T1.4 Where filtration is used— Non-reporting N/A (a) the filtration system must be operated within the manufacturer’s design specifications at all times; and (b) pumps must not be connected directly to the discharge side of any cartridge filter; and (c) where pumping occurs after filtration, the filtrate must first pass directly to a tank. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=23\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":24,\"text\":\"T1.5 All water passing through a treatment plant must be Non-reporting N/A disinfected with UV light and UV units must— (a) deliver at least 40 mJ/cm2 (or equivalent) reduction equivalent dose (RED) of UV light; and (b) be installed, maintained and operated according to the manufacturer’s instructions; and (c) be certified to (and operate within the specifications of) at least one of the following standards unless purchased before 1 August 2022: (i) NSF/ANSI 55 Class A (NSF, ANSI n.d): (ii) Ultraviolet Disinfection Guidance Manual (USEPA 2006b): (iii) DVGW Technical Standard W294 (DVGW 2006): (iv) ÖNORM M 5873-1: 2020 01 01; or ÖNORM M5873 (Osterreichisches Normungsinstitut 2001): (v) DIN 19294-1:2020-08. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 T1.6- Revoked T1.8 Rules revoked on 29 November 2024 Table 10.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=24\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":24,\"text\":\"Removed 16 17 18 16 Removed 17 Removed 18 Removed\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=24\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":25,\"text\":\"4.5 D1 Distribution System Rules Module Rule Requirement Rule type Compliance number period D1.1 Water in a distribution zone must be monitored at least Monitoring 3 Months every 3 months for the following: (a) E. coli: (b) total coliforms.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=25\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":25,\"text\":\"Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 D1.2 The following measures must be taken in relation to Non-reporting N/A backflow: (a) each distribution zone must be assessed for backflow risks and cross-connections at least every two years: (b) a register of all points of supply where there is a medium or high backflow risk must be maintained: (c) a suitable backflow prevention device must be installed at any point of supply identified in the register: (d) every testable backflow prevention device must be inspected and tested at least every 2 years by a suitably trained and qualified person: (e) any faulty backflow prevention device must be remediated as soon as practicable: (f) records of backflow prevention device test results must be retained. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 Table 11. Removed 19 19 Removed\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=25\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":26,\"text\":\"4.6 S2 Source Water Rules Module Rule Requirement Rule type Compliance number period S2.1 Surface and groundwater sources must be monitored— Monitoring 1 Month (a) at least every month for the following: (i) E. coli: (ii) total coliforms; and (b) at least annually for the following: Monitoring 1 Year (i) pH: (ii) turbidity: (iii) iron: (iv) manganese: (v) nitrate: (vi) arsenic: (vii) boron. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 S2.2 Roof water sources must be monitored— Monitoring 1 Month (a) at least every month for the following: (i) E. coli: (ii) total coliforms; and (b) at least every 3 years for the following: Monitoring 1 Year (i) cadmium: (ii) copper: (iii) lead.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=26\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":26,\"text\":\"Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 S2.3 Any chemical determinands that are identified as Monitoring 1 Year presenting a risk to the supply or are found to exceed 50% of their MAV in source water samples must be monitored at least annually until 3 consecutive results from source water samples are less than 50% of the MAV. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 S2.4 Samples collected under— Non-reporting N/A (a) rule S2.1 must be collected at the abstraction point or treatment plant prior to treatment and/or mixing with other sources; and (b) rule S2.2 must be collected at the raw water storage tank outlet and prior to any treatment. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=26\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":27,\"text\":\"S2.5 Water sources must be categorised as either no, low, Assurance 1 Year medium or high risk for the presence of cyanobacteria.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=27\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":27,\"text\":\"Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 S2.6 The following measures must be taken in relation to Non-reporting N/A surface water intakes: (a) each month between October and May (inclusive)— (i) the water and area around and upstream of the intake must be visually inspected for the presence of benthic cyanobacteria mats and planktonic cyanobacterial growth; or (ii) water must be monitored for cyanobacterial cell count at the treatment plant prior to mixing and treatment: (b) if there is evidence of cyanobacterial growth, steps must be taken to evaluate the cyanotoxin risk to consumers: (c) if there is a risk of supplying water with cyanotoxins that exceed MAVs, abstraction of water must be discontinued, an alternative source used, or treatment installed, until the risk is no longer present.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=27\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":27,\"text\":\"Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 S2.7 Consumer taste or odour complaints, which have the Assurance 1 Year potential to relate to cyanobacteria, must be— (a) recorded; and (b) investigated to determine the cause. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 Table 12. Removed 20 21 Table 13. Removed 20 Removed 21 Removed\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=27\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":28,\"text\":\"4.7 T2 Treatment Rules Module Rule Requirement Rule Type Compliance Number period T2.1 Water leaving a treatment plant must be monitored at Monitoring 1 Month least every month for the following: (a) E. coli: (b) total coliforms: (c) any chemical used in the treatment process (Fluoride and FAC have separate requirements, see rule T2.2). Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 T2.2 Water leaving the treatment plant must be monitored Monitoring 1 Month at least 8 times per month for the following: (a) turbidity: (b) FAC (not required for self-supplied buildings): (c) pH (not required for self-supplied buildings): (d) fluoride (only if added to the water).\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=28\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":28,\"text\":\"Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 T2.3 Samples collected for rule T2.2 must— Non-reporting N/A (a) be evenly spaced across each month; and (b) use at least 3 different days of the week within each month. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 T2.4 Water leaving a treatment plant must be monitored for Monitoring 3 Months chlorate at least every 3 months if sodium hypochlorite (or calcium hypochlorite) is used as a disinfectant and this solution is not generated on-site from a salt brine solution. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 T2.5 Any additional determinands which are identified as Monitoring 1 Year presenting a risk to the supply must be monitored at least every month in water leaving a treatment plant until 3 consecutive results from treated water samples are less than the 50% of the MAV. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=28\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":29,\"text\":\"T2.6 Water leaving a treatment plant must meet the Assurance 1 Year following limits while the plant is in production: (a) turbidity must be less than 5 NTU: (b) FAC must be at least 0.5 mg/L (not required for self-supplied buildings): (c) pH must be between 6.5 – 8 (not required for self-supplied buildings). Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 T2.7 All water, excluding groundwater abstracted from a Assurance 1 Year depth of greater than 30 metres, must be filtered by a— (a) cartridge filter system that includes a 5 micron (nominal) or smaller pore size; or (b) back-washable media filter; or (c) slow sand (biological) filter; or (d) membrane filter.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=29\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":29,\"text\":\"Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 T2.8 If cartridge filtration is used— Assurance 1 Year (a) the final cartridge must have a pore size of 5 microns (nominal) or less; and (b) pumps must not be connected directly to the discharge side of any cartridge filter; and (c) where pumping occurs after filtration, the filtrate must first discharge directly to a tank; and (d) differential pressure must be measured across each cartridge filter and must not exceed the cartridge filter manufacturer’s specifications; and (e) the flow through any filters must be within the manufacturer’s design specifications for the treatment processes at all times. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 T2.9 All water supplied to consumers must be— Assurance 1 Year (a) disinfected with UV light; and (b) disinfected with chlorine (not required for self- supplied buildings).\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=29\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":29,\"text\":\"Rule replaced on 29 November 2024\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=29\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":30,\"text\":\"T2.10 The following requirements must be met for UV Assurance 1 Year treatment: (a) UV units must provide at least 40 mJ/cm2 (or equivalent) reduction equivalent dose (RED) of UV light: (b) UVT must be monitored monthly or continuously: (c) UVI or UV dose must be recorded twice weekly or monitored continuously: (d) UV units must be certified to (and operate within the specifications of) at least one of the following: (i) NSF/ANSI 55 Class A (NSF, ANSI n.d): (ii) Ultraviolet Disinfection Guidance Manual (USEPA 2006b): (iii) DVGW Technical Standard W294 (DVGW 2006): (iv) ÖNORM M 5873-1: 2020 01 01; or ÖNORM M5873 (Osterreichisches Normungsinstitut 2001): (v) DIN 19294-1:2020-08: (e) water flowing through the UV unit/s must— (i) meet the limits of UVT specified by the manufacturer; and (ii) be restricted or monitored so that the flow rate does not exceed the flow rate specified by the manufacturer: (f) lamp usage must— (i) be recorded; and (ii) be alarmed if the UV unit has more than one lamp; and (iii) not exceed manufacturer's recommendations: (g) the duty UVI sensor must— (i) be referenced against a new sensor annually; and (ii) be replaced if the duty sensor reads levels different to the new sensor that are outside the manufacturer’s recommendation.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=30\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":30,\"text\":\"Rule replaced on 29 November 2024\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=30\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":31,\"text\":\"T2.11- Revoked 22 23 24 25 26 27 T2.25 Rules revoked on 29 November 2024 Table 14. Removed 22 Removed 23 Removed 24 Removed 25 Removed 26 Removed 27 Removed\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=31\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":32,\"text\":\"4.8 D2 Distribution System Rules Module Rule Requirement Rule type Compliance number period D2.1 Water in each distribution zone must be monitored at Monitoring 1 Month least every month for the following: (a) E. coli: (b) total coliforms. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 D2.2 Water in each distribution zone must be monitored at Monitoring 1 Month least 8 times every month for FAC. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 D2.3 Samples for FAC, E. coli and total coliforms must be Non-reporting N/A taken at regular sampling points that are representative of the geographical coverage of each distribution zone including— (a) exit points of storage reservoirs/tanks; and (b) end points of the distribution zone.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=32\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":32,\"text\":\"Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 D2.4 Monitoring for FAC in each distribution zone must— Non-reporting N/A (a) be evenly spaced across each month; and (b) use different days of the week. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 D2.5 Water in each distribution zone must be monitored at Monitoring 1 Year least annually for the following: (a) antimony: (b) cadmium: (c) chromium: (d) copper: (e) lead: (f) mercury: (g) nickel. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 D2.6 When sampling for metals, sample points must— Non-reporting N/A (a) be flushed immediately prior to obtaining samples; and (b) not be disinfected, e.g. flamed, immediately prior to sampling for metals; and (c) be located near the end point of the system. Rule replaced on 29 November 2024\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=32\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":33,\"text\":\"D2.7 FAC in each distribution zone must be: Assurance 1 Year (a) at least 0.2 mg/L in 80% of samples taken; and (b) no less than 0.1 mg/L at all times.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=33\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":33,\"text\":\"Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 D2.8 The following measures must be taken in relation to Assurance 1 Year backflow: (a) each distribution zone must be assessed for backflow risks and cross-connections at least annually: (b) a register of all connections where there is a medium or high backflow risk must be maintained: (c) a suitable backflow prevention device must be installed at any connection identified in the register: (d) every testable backflow prevention device must be inspected and tested annually by a suitably trained and qualified person: (e) any faulty backflow prevention device must be remediated as soon as practicable: (f) records of backflow prevention device test results must be retained for at least 3 years. Rule inserted on 29 November 2024 Table 15. Removed 28 28 Removed\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=33\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":34,\"text\":\"4.9 S3 Source Water Rules Module 4.9.1 S3 Source Water Protozoa Log Credit Treatment Requirements Drinking water suppliers must determine the level of protozoa treatment required for a drinking water supply based on the requirements set out in the source class classifications below. Treatment systems must provide a protozoa barrier equal to or exceeding the source water, log credit treatment requirements for the applicable source water class. Class 1 – Protozoa barrier not required.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=34\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":34,\"text\":\"Criteria Groundwater sources (bore water) that draw water from a depth of more than 30 metres below ground level (with depth measured from ground level to the top of the upper most screen) and via a sanitary bore head in which E. coli and total coliforms have not been detected over a period of three years 29 (monthly samples with a maximum of 45 days between samples). If a groundwater source has demonstrated Class 1 status but any result for E. coli or total coliforms is positive, the groundwater source must assume Interim Class 1 status.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=34\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":34,\"text\":\"Interim Class 1 If a drinking water supplier intends to demonstrate Class 1 status for a groundwater source (bore water) but does not have the required E. coli or total coliforms data, they may demonstrate Interim Class 1 status by monitoring E. coli and total coliforms in bore water 30 daily for 36 days, and then weekly until 52 weeks of data has accrued with no E. coli or total coliforms detected. If any sample for E. coli or total coliforms is positive, the process must begin again until 52 weeks of data has accrued with negative results. If Class 1 category cannot be established within 24 months of beginning sampling, the water source must be classified as Class 2.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=34\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":34,\"text\":\"Class 2 – Protozoa barrier – 3 log Criteria Groundwater sources that draw water from a depth of between 30 metres and 10 metres below ground level (with depth measured from ground level to the top of the upper most screen). 29 Samples can be from individual bores or water combined from up to six bores if the water is from the same aquifer and has similar characteristics. 30 Samples can be from individual bores or water combined from up to six bores if the water is from the same aquifer and has similar characteristics.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=34\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":35,\"text\":\"Class 3 – Protozoa Barrier – 4 log Criteria Groundwater sources that draw water from a depth of less than 10 metres below ground level (with depth measured from ground level to the top of the upper most screen), groundwater sources that draw water from a depth of 10 or more metres below ground level without a sanitary bore head, spring water sources and surface water sources. Class 4 – Protozoa Barrier – 3 log Criteria Drinking water supplies that require a minimum protozoa treatment barrier of 4-log may reduce the level of protozoa treatment to a minimum of 3-log if the source water risk management plan for the supply provides evidence that the source water has a low risk of protozoa contamination. 4.9.2 S3 Sanitary Bore Head Requirements A bore head is considered a sanitary bore head if it meets all of the following criteria: 1.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=35\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":35,\"text\":\"The bore head is installed above ground. 2. The bore is installed in an area of ground that is not below the surrounding ground level such that ponding could occur around the bore head during rainfall. 3. The annulus of the casing is sealed taking account of the formation that the bore has been installed in, to prevent the ingress of surface water via the outside of the casing and the bore is grouted below ground to an appropriate depth. 4. A concrete apron is installed around the bore head, extending a minimum of one metre in all directions from the casing and sloping away from the casing so that any water on the ground surface is carried away from the bore. 5. All apertures into the bore (for cables etc) are sealed and watertight to prevent access from water and vermin-proofed to prevent access by small animals etc. 6.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=35\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":35,\"text\":\"All air vents and any other apertures that are not watertight must be screened to prevent access by small animals, face downwards, and be elevated at least 0.5 metres above the surrounding ground level. 7. Reasonable security measures are in place to protect the bore head from unauthorised access or interference. 8. If the bore head is in an area where farm animals are present, it must be fenced to exclude those animals from an area extending at least five metres in all directions from the bore head. 9. A mechanism prevents backflow at the bore head. 10. The bore head is inspected monthly for damage or defects and records kept of all inspections for at least five years.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=35\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":36,\"text\":\"4.9.3 S3 Source Water Monitoring Rules Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period S3.1 Water suppliers must determine the class of source Assurance 1 Year water for each of the source waters that are used, based on the Source Water Protozoa Log Credit Treatment Requirements. S3.2 A drinking water supplier demonstrating Class 1 or Assurance 1 Year Interim Class 1 groundwater must document whether or not the bore meets the requirements of a sanitary bore head. S3.3 All source waters must be monitored for the Monitoring N/A determinands and at the frequencies set out in Table 16 and Table 18. S3.4 All groundwaters must be monitored for the Monitoring N/A radiological determinands set out in Table 17.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=36\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":36,\"text\":\"S3.5 Additional monitoring of Class 2, Class 3 and Class 4 Monitoring N/A source waters must be undertaken either during or immediately after a severe or extreme weather event or other events which could adversely affect source water quality. 31 32 S3.6 Monitoring of source water must be undertaken for Monitoring As determined any determinand additional to those set out in Table by the Source 16 and 18 if the determinand has been identified in Water Risk the drinking water supply Source Water Risk Management Management Plan as presenting a potential risk to Plan the drinking water supply. S3.7 Water sources must be categorised as either low-risk, Assurance 1 Year medium-risk or high-risk for the presence of cyanobacteria.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=36\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":36,\"text\":\"S3.8 When a water supply is categorised as medium or Assurance 1 Year high-risk under rule S3.7, a cyanobacteria / cyanotoxin response plan must be prepared which includes vigilance levels for assessing the presence of cyanobacteria and alert levels related to the presence of cyanotoxins, monitoring for cyanobacteria/cyanotoxins and the action that will be taken to protect consumers. 31 Samples of raw water may be collected at the treatment plant for this purpose. 32 Samples should not be collected if there are health and safety risks to people collecting samples that are not appropriately eliminated or minimised.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=36\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":37,\"text\":\"S3.9 If a water supplier becomes aware of the presence of Assurance 1 Year cyanobacteria in source water, monitoring to determine the level of cyanobacteria and/or cyanotoxin levels must be considered in accordance with the cyanobacteria/cyanotoxin response plan. Table 16. S3 Source Water Monitoring Determinands Determinand Determinands monitored at each abstraction point 33 Sampling Group 34 Frequency Bacterial E. coli and total coliforms 2 per Month Physico- Iron, manganese, colour, nitrate Monthly chemical Alkalinity, antimony, arsenic, barium, cadmium, calcium, Annually 35 chloride, chromium, copper, lead, magnesium, mercury, nickel, sodium, sulphate. Table 17.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=37\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":37,\"text\":\"S3 Class 1, Class 2 and Class 3 Groundwater Source Monitoring Determinands Determinand Determinands monitored at each source 36 37 Sampling Group Frequency Radiological Gross alpha activity Every ten years Gross beta activity Chemical Potassium 38 Every ten years 33 Samples may be collected either at the source abstraction point or at the treatment plant before any form of treatment, if water is from a single source. If multiple sources are used, samples must be collected from each source at points before any mixing of source water occurs. 34 Where multiple bores access the same aquifer, one bore can be sampled to provide results that are representative of a number of bores if the water supplier can demonstrate that the bore that is sampled is representative of the bores that are not sampled.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=37\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":37,\"text\":\"The representative nature of the sampled bore must be re- established every five years or after significant seismic activity. 35 Must be sampled monthly if the determinand exceeds 50% of its MAV. Sampling may return to annually after 12 consecutive samples are less than 50% of the MAV. 36 Samples may be collected either at the source abstraction point or at the treatment plant before any form of treatment, if water is from a single source. If multiple sources are used, samples must be collected from each source at points before any mixing of source water occurs. 37 Where multiple bores access the same aquifer, one bore can be sampled to provide results that are representative of a number of bores if the water supplier can demonstrate that the bore that is sampled is representative of the bores that are not sampled.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=37\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":37,\"text\":\"38 When samples are analysed for alpha and beta emitting particles a sample for potassium must also be analysed so that the potassium 40 contribution to beta emitting particles can be determined.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=37\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":38,\"text\":\"Table 18. S3 Raw Water Monitoring Parameters Parameters monitored in raw water from each source or combined Sampling sources 39 Frequency Class 1 and Interim Class 1 Physico- Conductivity, pH, Turbidity Monthly for 12 chemical months Every 6 months if there is no significant variation in the initial 12 months of samples 40 Classes 2, 3 and 4 Physico- Conductivity, pH, Turbidity Continuous 41 chemical 39 If water is abstracted from more than one source and combined at the treatment plant, only the combined water needs to be analysed and this can be done at the treatment plant before any form of treatment. 40 If a six monthly sample varies significantly from previous samples, sampling should return to monthly for 12 months.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=38\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":38,\"text\":\"41 Where continuous monitoring analysers fail or require maintenance, daily grab samples can be taken until the continuous monitoring equipment can be brought back into service.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=38\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":39,\"text\":\"4.10 T3 Treatment Rules Module 4.10.1 T3 Bacterial Rules One or more of the following options must be used to demonstrate bacterial compliance: 1. Disinfection with chlorine 42 2. Disinfection with chlorine dioxide 3. Disinfection with ozone 4. Disinfection with UV light 4.10.1.1 T3 Bacterial Rules for Water Disinfected with Chlorine Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period T3.1 All water passing through the treatment plant must be Monitoring 1 Day treated with chlorine and must be monitored in accordance with Table 19. T3.2 Treated water must achieve a chlorine C.t value of at Monitoring 1 Day least 15 min.mg/L for at least 95 % of each day. T3.3 Treated water must have a FACE of no less than 0.2 Monitoring 1 Day mg/L. T3.4 T10 contact time of at least 5 minutes must be Monitoring 1 Day demonstrated.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=39\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":39,\"text\":\"T3.5 Turbidity of water leaving the treatment plant must be Monitoring 1 Day less than 1.0 NTU for at least 95% of each day. 43 T3.6 Turbidity must not exceed 2.0 NTU for the duration of Monitoring 1 Day any consecutive 15-minute period. Table 19. T3 Requirements for Drinking Water Disinfected with Chlorine Parameters that need to be Parameters: FAC continuously monitored and Where it needs to be monitored: Water at a point after the where they need to be prescribed disinfection contact time has elapsed. monitored: Parameters: pH Where it needs to be monitored: Water at a point after the prescribed disinfection contact time has elapsed. 42 For Self-supplied Buildings supplies that provide water to more than a single building chlorine must be used to demonstrate bacterial compliance.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=39\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":39,\"text\":\"43 Where lime is used for post-treatment pH adjustment, analysis may be undertaken before the lime is dosed.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=39\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":40,\"text\":\"Parameters: Turbidity Where it needs to be monitored: Water at a point after the prescribed disinfection contact time has elapsed. Parameters: Flow Where it needs to be monitored: Water leaving the contact tank unless there is a high-level outlet weir in which case water entering the contact tank is acceptable. A calculated outlet flow based on inlet flow and contact level change is also an acceptable approach. Water level in the contact tank (if used). Values that need to be FACE. continuously monitored: T10 Contact Time. C.t\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=40\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":41,\"text\":\"4.10.1.2 T3 Bacterial Rules for Water Disinfected with Chlorine Dioxide Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period T3.7 All water passing through the treatment plant must be Monitoring 1 Day treated with chlorine dioxide and must be monitored in accordance with Table 19. T3.8 Treated water must achieve a chlorine C.t value of at Monitoring 1 Day least 15 min.mg/L for at least 95 % of each day. T3.9 T10 contact time of at least 5 minutes must be Monitoring 1 Day demonstrated. T3.10 Turbidity of water leaving the treatment plant must be Monitoring 1 Day less than 1.0 NTU for at least 95% of each day. 44 T3.11 Turbidity must not exceed 2.0 NTU for the duration of Monitoring 1 Day any consecutive 15-minute period. Table 20.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=41\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":41,\"text\":\"T3 Requirements for Drinking Water Disinfected with Chlorine Dioxide Parameters that need to be Parameters: Chlorine dioxide continuously monitored and Where it needs to be monitored: Water at a point after the where they need to be prescribed disinfection contact time has elapsed. monitored: Parameters: FAC if used in combination with chlorine dioxide Where it needs to be monitored: Water at a point after the prescribed disinfection contact time has elapsed. Parameters: pH Where it needs to be monitored: Water at a point after the prescribed disinfection contact time has elapsed. Parameters: Turbidity Where it needs to be monitored: Water at a point after the prescribed disinfection contact time has elapsed.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=41\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":41,\"text\":\"Parameters: Flow Where it needs to be monitored: Water leaving the contact tank unless there is a high-level outlet weir in which case water entering the contact tank is acceptable. A calculated outlet flow based on inlet flow and contact level change is also an acceptable approach. Water level in the contact tank (if used) 44 Where lime is used for pH adjustment, samples may be taken before the lime is dosed.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=41\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":42,\"text\":\"Values that need to be FACE if chlorine is used in combination with chlorine dioxide. continuously monitored: Total disinfectant T10 Contact Time. C.t 4.10.1.3 T3 Bacterial Rules for Water Disinfected with Ozone Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period T3.12 All water passing through the treatment plant must Monitoring 1 Day pass through the ozone contactor and must be monitored in accordance with Table 21. T3.13 C.t of at least 1.2 mg.min/L for more than 95% of each Monitoring 1 Day day must be achieved. T3.14 Turbidity must not exceed 5.0 NTU for the duration of Monitoring 1 Day any consecutive 15-minute period. Table 21. T3 Requirements for Water Disinfected with Ozone Parameters that need to be Parameters: Ozone Residual continuously monitored and Where it needs to be monitored: Water leaving the contact tank.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=42\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":42,\"text\":\"45 where they need to be Parameters: Turbidity monitored: Where it needs to be monitored: Water at a point immediately before or after the contact tank. Parameters: Flow Where it needs to be monitored: Water leaving the contact tank unless there is a high-level outlet weir in which case water entering the contact tank is acceptable. A calculated outlet flow based on inlet flow and contact level change is also an acceptable approach. Level of water in the contact tank (if used). Values that need to be T10 Contact Time. continuously monitored: C.t (Ozone x T10 Contact Time). 45 Additional monitoring sites may be used in addition to the contact tank exit point if the water supplier can demonstrate that they improve the accuracy of the monitoring information.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=42\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":43,\"text\":\"4.10.1.4 T3 Bacterial Rules for Water Disinfected with Ultraviolet Light Rule Requirement Monitoring/ Compliance Number Assurance period T3.15 All water passing through the treatment plant must Monitoring 1 Day pass through the UV reactor(s) and be within the reactor’s certified flow range and must be monitored in accordance with Table 22. T3.16 A reduction equivalent dose (RED) of not less than 40 Monitoring 1 Day mJ/cm2 (or equivalent) must be achieved for not less than 95 % of each day. T3.17 The RED UV dose must be not less than 40 mJ/cm2 for Monitoring 1 Day any consecutive 15-minute period. T3.18 Turbidity must not exceed 5.0 NTU for the duration of Monitoring 1 Day any consecutive 15-minute period.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=43\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":43,\"text\":\"T3.19 For UV units certified to Ultraviolet Disinfection Assurance 1 Year Guidance Manual (USEPA 2006b) monitored UVI, UVT and flow must be used to calculate dose. T3.20 For UV units certified to DVGW Technical Standard Assurance 1 Year W294 (DVGW 2006) or ÖNORM M 5873-1: 2020 01 01 46 monitored flow must be used for UVI control or the reactor run at full power. UVI and UVT must be more than the validated value for the respective flow. T3.21 For UV units certified to NSF/ANSI 55 Class A (NSF, Assurance 1 Year ANSI n.d.); (for populations of up to 5000) – 3-log a fault must be generated when any parameter exceeds the certified value. 46 Or UV reactors certified to öNORM M5873 (Osterreichisches Normungsinstitut 2001)\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=43\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":44,\"text\":\"Table 22. T3 Requirements for UV Disinfection Parameters that need to be Parameters: UVT continuously monitored and Where it needs to be monitored: Water entering or leaving the where they need to be UV reactor(s). 47 monitored: Parameters: Turbidity Where it needs to be monitored: Water entering or leaving the UV reactor(s). Can include upstream filter monitoring if there is no other process between the filters and the UV reactors. Parameters: UVI or dose Where it needs to be monitored: The same point in the reactor as that used for certification/validation. Parameters: Flow Where it needs to be monitored: At a point representative of water entering or leaving the reactor(s). UVI sensor checking and Duty UVI sensors must be checked at least monthly against the standardisation: reference sensor.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=44\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":44,\"text\":\"If the difference between the two readings exceeds the manufacturer’s specified limits, then the Duty UVI sensor must be replaced. The reference UVI sensor must be standardised at least annually in accordance with Ultraviolet Disinfection Guidance Manual (USEPA 2006b) or other traceable procedure. Alternatively, after 12 months the drinking water supplier can use the reference sensor as a duty sensor and purchase a new standardised sensor for use as a reference sensor. UV certification/validation: The equipment must be certified and operated to meet the RED dose of 40 mJ/cm2 (or equivalent) using at least one of the: Ultraviolet Disinfection Guidance Manual (USEPA 2006b). DVGW Technical Standard W294 (DVGW 2006) ÖNORM M 5873-1: 2020 01 01. 48 NSF/ANSI 55 (NSF, ANSI nd) for Class A systems (for populations of up to 5000) – 3-log.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=44\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":44,\"text\":\"47 Does not apply to UV units certified to NSF/ANSI 55 (NSF, ANSI nd) for Class A systems (for populations of up to 5000) – 3-log. 48 UV reactors installed before 1 January 2020 can be certified to öNORM M5873 (Osterreichisches Normungsinstitut 2001).\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=44\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":45,\"text\":\"4.10.2 T3 Protozoal Rules Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period T3.22 Drinking water supplies must have a protozoa Assurance 1 Year barrier that provides treatment equal to or exceeding the log level of the water class identified by the Source Water Protozoa Log Credit Treatment Requirements. Treatment processes and log credit allocations The processes that can be used for protozoal treatment, and the log credits that can be achieved are: 1. Coagulation, flocculation and sedimentation process without filtration [0.5-Log] 2. Coagulation, flocculation and direct filtration [2.5-3.5-Log] 3. Coagulation, flocculation, sedimentation, and filtration [3-4-Log] 4. Second stage filtration [0.5-Log] 5. Slow sand filtration [2.5-Log] 6. Membrane filtration [up to 4.0-Log] 7. Cartridge filtration [2.0-Log] 8.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=45\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":45,\"text\":\"Ozone [0.25 to 3.0-Log] 9. Ultraviolet light [up to 4-Log] The cumulative log credits of a process that includes filtration or sedimentation and a disinfection process can be calculated by adding the log credits of the qualifying processes that are used. If two filtration processes are used, the second filtration process is considered to be second stage filtration and can add only 0.5-Log to the cumulative log credits. Water suppliers must comply with the rules set out below that apply to the treatment process(es) they are using.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=45\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":46,\"text\":\"4.10.2.1 T3 Protozoal Rules for Coagulation, Flocculation and Sedimentation without Filtration [0.5-Log]. Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period T3.23 All water passing through the treatment plant Assurance 1 Year must pass through the coagulation, flocculation and sedimentation process. T3.24 The sedimentation process must achieve at least a Monitoring 1 Day 70% reduction in raw water turbidity each day, based on the arithmetic mean of the turbidity of the raw water and the water leaving the sedimentation process. T3.25 All of the monitoring requirements in Table 23 Monitoring 1 Day must be met. Table 23.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=46\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":46,\"text\":\"T3 Requirements for Coagulation, Flocculation and Sedimentation without Filtration Parameters that need to be Parameter: Turbidity. continuously monitored and Where it needs to be monitored: On the inlet and outlet of where they need to be sedimentation process. monitored: Process Limitations: Sedimentation includes dissolved air flotation. Water from any treatment processes must not be added to the raw water and recycled through the plant.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=46\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":47,\"text\":\"4.10.2.2 T3 Protozoal Rules for Coagulation, Flocculation, and Direct Filtration [2.5-Log] Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period T3.26 All water passing through the treatment plant must Assurance 1 Year pass through the coagulation, flocculation, and filtration process. T3.27 Turbidity must not exceed 0.3 NTU for more than 5% Monitoring 1 Day of each day. T3.28 Turbidity must not exceed 0.5 NTU for the duration of Monitoring 1 Day any consecutive 15-minute period. T3.29 All of the requirements in Table 24 must be met.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=47\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":47,\"text\":\"Assurance 1 Year 4.10.2.3 T3 Protozoal Rules for Coagulation, Flocculation, and Direct Filtration [3.0-Log] Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period T3.30 All water passing through the treatment plant must Assurance 1 Year pass through the coagulation, flocculation, and filtration process. T3.31 Turbidity must not exceed 0.15 NTU for more than 5% Monitoring 1 Day of each day. T3.32 Turbidity must not exceed 0.5 NTU for the duration of Monitoring 1 Day any consecutive 15-minute period. T3.33 All of the requirements in Table 24 must be met. Assurance 1 Year\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=47\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":48,\"text\":\"4.10.2.4 T3 Protozoal Rules for Coagulation, Flocculation, and Direct Filtration [3.5-Log] Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period T3.34 All water passing through the treatment plant Assurance 1 Year must pass through the coagulation, flocculation and filtration process. T3.35 Turbidity must not exceed 0.1 NTU for more than Monitoring 1 Day 5% of each day. T3.36 Turbidity must not exceed 0.3 NTU for the Monitoring 1 Day duration of any consecutive 15-minute period. T3.37 All of the monitoring requirements in Table 24 Monitoring 1 Day must be met. Table 24.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=48\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":48,\"text\":\"T3 Requirements for Coagulation, Flocculation and Direct Filtration Parameters that need to be Parameter: Turbidity continuously monitored and Where it needs to be monitored: On the outlet of each filter. where they need to be Monitored when the filter is in service to supply, on any recycle monitored: streams to the plant inlet (if present). Parameter: Service State. Where it needs to be monitored: Each filter. Process Limitations: Filtration is of a rapid media design (pressure or gravity equivalent).\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=48\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":48,\"text\":\"Water treatment plants that recycle waste streams (excluding water from rapid media ﬁlters being diverted during restart after backwash, often called ‘ﬁlter to waste’) must return the recycle stream so that it undergoes the full treatment process and provide ﬂow equalisation such that the instantaneous total return rate does not exceed 10% of the plant inﬂow. Turbidity monitoring is required to demonstrate that the recycle water has received effective solids/liquid separation. If instantaneous total return rate flows of greater than 10% of the plant inflow are recycled, separate treatment of the recycled stream is required to inactivate or kill protozoa and bacteria before the waste stream is returned to the plant inlet.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=48\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":49,\"text\":\"4.10.2.5 T3 Protozoal Rules for Coagulation, Flocculation, Sedimentation, and Filtration [3-Log] Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period T3.38 All water passing through the treatment plant must Assurance 1 Year pass through the coagulation, flocculation, sedimentation, and filtration process. T3.39 Turbidity must not exceed 0.3 NTU for more than Monitoring 1 Day 5% of each day. T3.40 Turbidity must not exceed 0.5 NTU for the duration Monitoring 1 Day of any consecutive 15-minute period. T3.41 All of the monitoring in Table 25 must be met.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=49\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":49,\"text\":\"Monitoring 1 Day 4.10.2.6 T3 Protozoal Rules for Coagulation, Flocculation, Sedimentation, and Filtration [3.5- Log] Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period T3.42 All water passing through the treatment plant must Assurance 1 Year pass through the coagulation, flocculation, sedimentation, and filtration process. T3.43 Turbidity must not exceed 0.15 NTU for more than Monitoring 1 Day 5% of each day. T3.44 Turbidity must not exceed 0.5 NTU for the duration Monitoring 1 Day of any consecutive 15-minute period. T3.45 All of the monitoring requirements in Table 25 must Monitoring 1 Day be met.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=49\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":50,\"text\":\"4.10.2.7 T3 Protozoal Rules for Coagulation, Flocculation, Sedimentation, and Filtration [4-Log] Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period T3.46 All water passing through the treatment plant Assurance 1 Year must pass through the coagulation, flocculation, sedimentation, and filtration process. T3.47 Turbidity must not exceed 0.1 NTU for more than Monitoring 1 Day 5% of each day. T3.48 Turbidity must not exceed 0.3 NTU for the Monitoring 1 Day duration of any consecutive 15-minute period. T3.49 All of the monitoring requirements in Table 25 Monitoring 1 Day must be met. Table 25.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=50\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":50,\"text\":\"T3 Requirements for Coagulation, Flocculation, Sedimentation and Filtration Parameters that need to be Parameters: Turbidity. continuously monitored and Where it needs to be monitored: On the outlet of each filter. where they need to be Monitored when the filter is in service to supply, on any recycle monitored: streams to the plant inlet (if present). Parameters: Service State. Where it needs to be monitored: Each filter. Process Limitations: Sedimentation includes dissolved air flotation. Filtration is of a rapid media design (pressure or gravity equivalent).\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=50\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":50,\"text\":\"Water treatment plants that recycle waste streams (excluding water from rapid media ﬁlters being diverted during restart after backwash, often called ‘ﬁlter to waste’) must return the recycle stream so that it undergoes the full treatment process and provide ﬂow equalisation such that the instantaneous total return rate does not exceed 10% of the plant inﬂow. Turbidity monitoring is required to demonstrate that the recycle water has received effective solids/liquid separation. If flows of greater than 10% are recycled, separate treatment of the recycled stream is required to inactivate or kill protozoa and bacteria before the waste stream is returned to the plant inlet.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=50\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":51,\"text\":\"4.10.2.8 T3 Protozoal Rules for Second Stage Filtration [0.5-Log] Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period T3.50 All water passing through the treatment plant Assurance 1 Year must pass through the second stage filtration process. T3.51 Turbidity must not exceed 0.1 NTU for more Monitoring 1 Day than 5% of each day. T3.52 Turbidity must not exceed 0.3 NTU for the Monitoring 1 Day duration of any consecutive 15-minute period. T3.53 All of the monitoring requirements in Table 26 Monitoring 1 Day must be met. Table 26. T3 Requirements for Second Stage Filtration Parameters that need to be Parameters: Turbidity. continuously monitored and Where it needs to be monitored: On the outlet of each filter, where they need to be monitored when the filter is in service to supply. monitored: Parameters: Service State.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=51\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":51,\"text\":\"Each filter. Process Limitations: A second ﬁltration stage consists of media or granular activated carbon, other ﬁne grain media in a separate stage after granular media ﬁltration with preceding coagulation.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=51\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":52,\"text\":\"4.10.2.9 T3 Protozoal Rules for Slow Sand Filtration [2.5-Log] Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period T3.54 All water passing through the treatment plant Assurance 1 Year must pass through the slow sand filtration process. T3.55 The filter must not dry out. Assurance 1 Year T3.56 Disinfecting chemicals must not be dosed so Assurance 1 Year that they leave a residual disinfectant upstream of the ﬁlter beds. T3.57 Following filter maintenance, when a filter is Assurance 1 Year brought back into operation, water must be discharged to waste until the ﬁltration process has been demonstrated to be effective. T3.58 The filters must be operated at a surface Assurance 1 Year loading rate of less than 0.35 m3/m2/h. T3.59 The temperature of the water entering the Monitoring 1 Day ﬁlter must not drop below 6°C at any time.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=52\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":52,\"text\":\"T3.60 Turbidity must not exceed 0.5 NTU for more Monitoring 1 Day than 5% of each day. T3.61 Turbidity must not exceed 1.0 NTU for the Monitoring 1 Day duration of any consecutive 15-minute period. T3.62 All of the monitoring requirements in Table 27 Monitoring 1 Day must be met. Table 27. T3 Requirements for Slow Sand Filtration Parameters that need to be Parameters: Temperature. continuously monitored and Where it needs to be monitored: Water entering the filtration stage. where they need to be Parameters: Turbidity. monitored: Where it needs to be monitored: On the outlet of each filter, monitored when the filter is in service to supply. Parameters: Flow. Where it needs to be monitored: On the outlet of each filter.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=52\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":52,\"text\":\"Values that need to be Surface loading rate. continuously monitored: Process Limitations: Water treatment plants that recycle waste streams must return the recycle stream so that it undergoes the full treatment process and provide ﬂow equalisation such that the instantaneous total return rate does not exceed 10% of the plant inﬂow.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=52\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":53,\"text\":\"Turbidity monitoring is required to demonstrate that the recycle water has received effective solids/liquid separation. If flows of greater than 10% are recycled, separate treatment of the recycled stream is required to inactivate or kill protozoa and bacteria before the waste stream is returned to the plant inlet. 4.10.2.10 T3 Protozoal Rules for Cartridge Filtration [2.0-Log] Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period T3.63 All water passing through the treatment plant Assurance 1 Year must pass through the cartridge filtration process. T3.64 Rapid pressure fluctuations on either side of the Assurance 1 Year cartridges must be avoided. Pumps must not be connected directly to the discharge side of a cartridge filter. After filtration, the ﬁltrate must pass to a tank if there is subsequent pumping.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=53\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":53,\"text\":\"T3.65 Filtered water turbidity does not exceed 1.0 NTU Monitoring 1 Day for more than 5% of each day. T3.66 Filtered water turbidity does not exceed 1.0 NTU Monitoring 1 Day for the duration of any consecutive 15-minute period. T3.67 The filtrate turbidity from each filter housing must Monitoring 1 Day not exceed the turbidity of the cartridge feed water for the duration of any consecutive 15- minute period. 49 T3.68 The equipment is operated within the ﬂow range Assurance 1 Year for which it was certified at all times. T3.69 Differential pressure across cartridges must be Assurance 1 Year measured and kept within the manufacturer’s recommendations at all times.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=53\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":53,\"text\":\"T3.70 The minimum differential pressure must always Assurance 1 Year exceed the differential pressure established at commissioning with a new cartridge and must be kept within the manufacturer’s recommendations. 49 Exceedances of not more than 2% are allowable to take account of measurement accuracy.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=53\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":54,\"text\":\"T3.71 Individual cartridges must be able to be clearly Assurance 1 Year identified in relation to the manufacturer and the part number that relates to the validation/certification. T3.72 All of the monitoring requirements in Table 28 Monitoring 1 Day must be met. Table 28. T3 Requirements for Cartridge Filtration Parameters that need to be Parameters: Turbidity. continuously monitored and Where it needs to be monitored: On the combined inlet to the where they need to be process. On the outlet of each filtration housing. monitored: Parameters: Differential Pressure. Where it needs to be monitored: Across each filtration housing. Parameters: Flow. Where it needs to be monitored: On the inlet or outlet of each filtration housing. Parameters: Service State. Where it needs to be monitored: Each filtration unit.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=54\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":54,\"text\":\"Cartridge Validation/ Each cartridge must have a certified Cryptosporidium (oo)cyst Certification: removal efficiency of at least 3-log and be certified to one of the following: 1. the USEPA (2010)’s Long Term 2 Enhanced Surface Water Treatment Rule: Toolbox Guidance Manual Part 8: Bag and Cartridge Filters. 2. the (oo)cyst reduction conditions of Drinking Water Treatment Units: Health effects, NSF/ANSI 53 (NSF, ANSI 2002). 3. The (oo)cyst removal requirements of a standard recognised by Taumata Arowai as being equivalent (e.g., AS/NZS 4348:1995 in conjunction with AS/NZS 3497:1998 (updated 2001)).\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=54\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":55,\"text\":\"4.10.2.11 T3 Protozoal Rules for Membrane Filtration [up to 4.0-Log] Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period T3.73 All water passing through the treatment plant Assurance 1 Year must pass through the membrane filtration process. T3.74 Direct integrity tests must be performed on each Assurance 1 Day membrane filtration unit at least daily (midnight to midnight) if the membrane filtration unit has been in service at any point during the day. T3.75 No membrane unit may be used while it has Assurance 1 Year failed its direct integrity test. T3.76 If the turbidity of the membrane filtrate exceeds Monitoring 1 Day 0.1 NTU for more than 15 consecutive minutes the membrane unit must be run to waste and not returned to supply until it has passed a direct integrity test.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=55\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":55,\"text\":\"T3.77 Filtrate turbidity must not exceed 1 NTU at any Monitoring 1 Day time. T3.78 If the membrane unit has been out of service for Assurance 1 Year maintenance or any other reason for more than 6 hours, a direct integrity test must be completed before the unit is returned to service. T3.79 All of the monitoring requirements in Table 29 Monitoring 1 Day must be met.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=55\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":56,\"text\":\"Table 29. T3 Requirements for Membrane Filtration Parameters that need to be Parameters: Turbidity. continuously monitored and Where it needs to be monitored: On the combined inlet to the where they need to be membrane process, 50 and on the outlet of each membrane filtration monitored: unit (rack, train, or cell) when in service to supply and on any recycle streams to the plant inlet (if present). Parameters: Service State. Where it needs to be monitored: Each membrane filtration unit. Parameters that need to be Parameters: Membrane Integrity. non-continuously monitored Where it needs to be monitored: Each membrane filtration unit. and where they need to be monitored: Process Limitations: Membrane filtration includes microfiltration and ultrafiltration.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=56\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":56,\"text\":\"Water treatment plants that recycle waste streams must return the recycle stream so that it is treated by the membrane filtration process and provide ﬂow equalisation such that the instantaneous total return rate does not exceed 10% of the plant inﬂow. Turbidity monitoring is required to demonstrate that the recycle water has received effective solids/liquid separation. If flows of greater than 10% are recycled, separate treatment of the recycled stream is required to inactivate or kill protozoa and bacteria before the waste stream is returned to the plant upstream of the membranes. Membrane Membranes must be certified to comply with NSF/ANSI 61: Drinking Validation/Certification: Water System Components – Health Effects and NSF/ANSI 419 Public Drinking Water Equipment Performance – Filtration or equivalent.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=56\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":56,\"text\":\"The maximum number of log credits (up to a maximum of 4.0) that a membrane ﬁltration process is eligible to receive for protozoa removal depends on third party certiﬁcation of the log removal that the ﬁlter plant can deliver. The manufacturer’s certiﬁcate (or validation) must specify the operational requirements under which the membrane units must be operated to meet the speciﬁcation and the integrity testing procedure that the water supplier must carry out to demonstrate that the plant is operating at the claimed log credit rating at 3-micron resolution. Direct Integrity test The test is applied in such a manner that a 3 μm hole affects the requirements: response from the test. The test can verify the log removal value claimed for the membrane process.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=56\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":56,\"text\":\"50 This may be source water turbidity before coagulation when membrane filtration is the only process. It may be the average of turbidity from in-service pre-treatment units.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=56\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":57,\"text\":\"4.10.2.12 T3 Protozoal Rules for Ozone Disinfection [0.25 to 3.0-Log] Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period T3.80 All water passing through the treatment plant Assurance 1 Year must pass through the ozone process. T3.81 The C.t and water temperature required for the Monitoring 1 Day log credit claimed (Table 30) must be achieved for more than 95% of each day. T3.82 The C.t and water temperature must not be Monitoring 1 Day less than 80% of the values required for the log credit claimed ( Table 30) for the duration of any consecutive 15-minute period. T3.83 Turbidity must not exceed 5.0 NTU for the Monitoring 1 Day duration of any consecutive 15-minute period. T3.84 All of the monitoring requirements in Table 31 Monitoring 1 Day must be met. Table 30.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=57\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":57,\"text\":\"T3 C.t values 51 (min.mg/L) for Cryptosporidium inactivation by ozone Log Credit Water Temperature (oC) 52 1 5 10 15 20 25 0.25 5.8 4.0 2.5 1.6 1.0 0.6 0.5 12 7.9 4.9 3.1 2.0 1.2 1.0 23 16 9.9 6.2 3.9 2.5 1.5 35 24 15 9.3 5.9 3.7 2.0 46 32 20 12 7.8 4.9 2.5 58 40 25 16 9.8 6.2 3.0 69 47 30 19 12 7.4 51 The C.t data in this table are valid for ozone concentrations in the range 0.2-5.0mg/L. 52 C.t values between the indicated temperatures may be determined by interpolation.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=57\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":58,\"text\":\"Table 31. T3 Requirements for Ozone Disinfection Parameters that need to be Parameters: Ozone residual. continuously monitored and Where it needs to be monitored: Water immediately before or after where they need to be the contact tank. 53 monitored: Parameters: Temperature. Where it needs to be monitored: Water leaving the contact tank. Parameters: Turbidity. Where it needs to be monitored: Water leaving the contact tank. Parameters: Flow. Where it needs to be monitored: Water leaving the contact tank unless there is a high-level outlet weir in which case water entering the contact tank is acceptable. A calculated outlet flow based on inlet flow and contact level change is also an acceptable approach. Water level in the contact tank (if used).\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=58\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":58,\"text\":\"Values that need to be T10 Contact Time. continuously monitored: C.t (Ozone x T10 Contact Time). 53 Additional monitoring sites may be used in addition to the contact tank entry or exit point if the water supplier can demonstrate that they improve the accuracy of the monitoring information.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=58\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":59,\"text\":\"4.10.2.13 T3 Protozoal Rules for Ultraviolet Light Disinfection [up to 4-Log as determined by the validation/certification of the reactor] Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period T3.85 All water passing through the treatment plant must Assurance 1 Year pass through the UV reactor(s) and be within the reactor’s certified flow range for at least 95% of each day. T3.86 The UV dose must meet or exceed that required to Monitoring 1 Day achieve the claimed log credit for at least 95% of each day. T3.87 The UV dose must not be less than that required to Monitoring 1 Day achieve the claimed log credit for the duration of any consecutive 15-minute period. T3.88 Turbidity must not exceed 5.0 NTU for the duration Monitoring 1 Day of any consecutive 15-minute period.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=59\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":59,\"text\":\"T3.89 UVT must meet or exceed 95% of the UVT for which Monitoring 1 Day the reactor has been certified for at least 95% of each day. 54 T3.90 UVT must not be less than 80% of the lowest UVT Monitoring 1 Day for which the reactor has been certified for the duration of any consecutive 15-minute period. 55 T3.91 All of the monitoring requirements in Monitoring 1 Day Table 32 must be met. 54 These requirements do not apply to UV disinfection systems that automatically adjust the UV dose as the UVT of the water flowing through the reactor varies. 55 These requirements do not apply to UV disinfection systems that automatically adjust the UV dose as the UVT of the water flowing through the reactor varies.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=59\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":60,\"text\":\"Table 32. T3 Requirements for UV Disinfection Parameters that need to be Parameters: UVT. continuously monitored and Where it needs to be monitored: Water entering or leaving the UV where they need to be reactor(s). 56 monitored: Parameters: Turbidity. Where it needs to be monitored: Water entering or leaving the UV reactor(s). Can include upstream filter monitoring if there is no other process between the filters and the UV reactors. Parameters: UVI or UV dose. For UV units certified to Ultraviolet Disinfection Guidance Manual (USEPA 2006b) monitored UVI, UVT and flow must be used to calculate dose. For UV units certified to DVGW Technical Standard W294 (DVGW 2006) or ÖNORM M 5873-1: 2020 01 01 57 monitored flow must be used for UVI control or the reactor run at full power.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=60\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":60,\"text\":\"UVI and UVT must be more than the validated value for the respective flow. For UV units certified to NSF/ANSI 55 Class A (NSF, ANSI n.d.); (for populations of up to 5000) – 3-log a fault must be generated when any parameter exceeds the certified value. Where it needs to be monitored: The same point in the reactor as that used for certification/validation. Parameters: Flow. Where it needs to be monitored: At a point representative of water entering or leaving the reactor(s). UVI sensor checking and Duty UVI sensors must be checked at least monthly against the standardisation: reference sensor. If the difference between the two readings exceeds the manufacturer’s specified limits, then the duty UVI sensor must be replaced.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=60\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":60,\"text\":\"Reference UVI sensor must be standardised at least annually in accordance with the Ultraviolet Disinfection Guidance Manual (USEPA 2006b) or other traceable procedure. Alternatively, after 12 months the supplier can use the reference sensor as a duty sensor and purchase a new standardised sensor for use as a reference sensor. Other instrumentation must be calibrated in accordance with the instrument manufacturer’s specified procedures and frequency. 56 These requirements do not apply to UV disinfection systems that automatically adjust the UV dose as the UVT of the water flowing through the reactor varies. 57 Or UV reactors certified to öNORM M5873 (Osterreichisches Normungsinstitut 2001).\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=60\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":61,\"text\":\"UV certification/validation: The equipment must be certified to meet the required log credit using at least one of: 1. the Ultraviolet Disinfection Guidance Manual (USEPA 2006b) – variable log credits. 2. DVGW Technical Standard W294 (DVGW 2006). 3. ÖNORM M 5873-1: 2020 01 01. 58 4. NSF/ANSI 55 (2019) for Class A systems (for populations of up to 5,000) – 3 log. 58 UV reactors installed before 1 January 2020 can be certified to öNORM M5873 (Osterreichisches Normungsinstitut 2001).\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=61\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":62,\"text\":\"4.10.3 T3 Chemical Rules Typical Value Range A drinking water supplier must sample the water leaving a treatment plant to determine the typical value range for specified chemical determinands to identify if any values exceed the MAV in the Drinking Water Standards and to determine the on-going monitoring frequency for those determinands. Standard typical range refers to a determined for which sample results are always less than 50% of the MAV. Elevated typical range refers to a determinand for which any sample exceeds 50% of the MAV but does not exceed 100% of the MAV. In the first 12 months of monitoring, 59 15 samples must be taken over a 12-month period (with no more than two samples collected in any calendar month) to determine the range of values for determinands in water leaving a treatment plant.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=62\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":62,\"text\":\"Values must be identified for determinands in the water leaving the treatment plant if: 1. the determinand has a MAV; and 2. the determinand is: a) present in the source water at a concentration more than 50% of its MAV (as determined by the supply source water risk management plan); and/or b) a chemical added during treatment, or a possible contaminant in a treatment chemical; and/or c) formed as the result of a treatment process and is not expected to change in concentration beyond the treatment plant. Following collection of the first 15 samples to establish the typical range of a determinand, the determinand must be monitored at the frequencies set out in Table 33. Some of the determinands that may arise from treatment chemicals are listed in Table 34.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=62\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":62,\"text\":\"Drinking water suppliers must test for determinands resulting from any other treatment chemicals that they use. If sodium hypochlorite is used, chlorate must be monitored weekly, regardless of the levels determined by sampling. 59 Where coagulants are used intermittently, the water supplier must undertake an appropriate sampling programme to determine the range of values for determinands in water leaving the treatment plant.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=62\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":63,\"text\":\"Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period T3.92 Values for determinands in treated water that: Assurance 1 year 1. exceed 50% of their MAV in the source water, or 2. are added or formed in the treatment process (as well as impurities in treatment chemicals), must be identified by the collection and analyses of 15 samples over a 12-month period (with no more than two samples collected in any calendar month). T3.93 Determinands identified by the sampling programme Monitoring 1 month outlined in rule T3.92 must be categorised as either standard typical range or elevated typical range and must be sampled at the frequency set out in Table 33. T3.94 Samples must be taken from a point directly after the Assurance 1 year final treatment process (including chlorine contact tanks as they are part of the treatment process).\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=63\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":63,\"text\":\"T3.95 Containers used for collecting samples must be Assurance 1 year obtained from a laboratory and appropriate for the target determinand. T3.96 Event based monitoring (determined by the water Monitoring As supplier) must be undertaken for any event that may determined rapidly introduce high concentrations of health- by the water significant chemical determinands into the water at the supplier source or at the treatment plant.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=63\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":64,\"text\":\"Table 33. T3 Treatment Chemical Determinand Minimum Sampling Frequencies Minimum sampling frequency Standard typical Elevated typical range Chlorate 60 FAC, Fluoride 61 range determinands determinands (Typical value < 50% (Value range 50% - MAV) 100% MAV) Annually Monthly Weekly 62 Continuous Table 34. T3 Possible Treatment Chemical Determinand Monitoring Treatment Chemical Determinand to monitor 63 Aluminium based coagulants/flocculants Aluminium, antimony, cadmium, copper, chromium, lead, mercury, nickel Iron based coagulants/flocculants Antimony, cadmium, copper, chromium, lead, mercury, nickel Polyacrylamide (polyelectrolyte) Acrylamide EP-DMA (polyelectrolyte) Epichlorohydrin Sodium hypochlorite Chlorate, Bromate Ozone Bromate Permanganate Manganese 60 If sodium hypochlorite is used for disinfection.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=64\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":64,\"text\":\"61 If fluoride is added as part of the treatment process. 62 Sampling may reduce to monthly if 12 consecutive samples are below 50% of the MAV. If a sample exceeds 50% of the MAV, sampling must return to weekly. 63 Monitoring is only required for treatment chemicals that are used or formed in a treatment process.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=64\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":65,\"text\":\"4.10.4 T3 Cyanotoxin Rules Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period T3.97 If cyanotoxins are identified in treated water, Monitoring 1 Month cyanotoxin testing must be undertaken in accordance with the supply cyanobacteria/cyanotoxin response plan or at least twice weekly (whichever is more frequent) until cyanotoxins are not present.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=65\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":66,\"text\":\"4.11 D3 Distribution System Rules Module 4.11.1 D3 Backflow Protection Rules Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period D3.1 Drinking water suppliers must prepare and Assurance 1 Year implement a backflow prevention programme to protect their distribution system against the risk of backflow. D3.2 Periodic surveys of backflow risks to a Assurance 1 Year distribution system to determine medium and high-risk sites must be undertaken by the drinking water supplier at least once every five years to assess the adequacy of backflow protection across the distribution system.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=66\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":66,\"text\":\"D3.3 Where backflow requirements at a point of Assurance 1 Year supply are deemed inadequate, the drinking water supplier must notify the local authority with details of the situation and risk, determine the backflow device that should be installed at the point of supply and ensure that it is installed in a timeframe commensurate with the risk but as soon as reasonably practicable. D3.4 Testing of all testable backflow prevention Assurance 1 Year devices installed at a point of supply specifically to protect the network (generally boundary devices) 64 must be undertaken, at least annually. D3.5 A drinking water supplier must maintain a Assurance 1 Year register of the location of all point of supply testable backflow protection devices, device types, assessed risk level and the results of testing of all devices.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=66\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":66,\"text\":\"64 Does not include devices that are installed downstream of the point of supply and/or within buildings that have the primary purpose of protecting building users.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=66\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":67,\"text\":\"D3.6 Access to a water network through use of a Assurance 1 Year standpipe is not permitted except by Fire and Emergency New Zealand, other emergency services, the drinking water supplier, or authorised contractors to the drinking water supplier where it is reasonably necessary to access the network for the operation of the drinking water supply. 4.11.2 D3 New and Repaired Watermains Hygiene Procedures Rules Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period D3.7 Before carrying out or commissioning repairs to Assurance 1 Year pipes in a water distribution system, a drinking water supplier must undertake and keep records of a risk assessment to determine the risk of contamination of the network and the procedures required to minimise that risk.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=67\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":67,\"text\":\"D3.8 All materials used in construction and repairs Assurance 1 Year must be free of visible contamination and remain protected from contamination until installation. D3.9 All tools contacting the water supply or its parts, Assurance 1 Year particularly cutting surfaces, must be adequately disinfected prior to commencing work and subsequently as necessary when tools contact soil or backfill material. D3.10 Disinfection of mains (when required) must Assurance 1 Year follow best management practices including but not limited to methods such as tablet, slug, spray chlorination, or equivalent as appropriate. D3.11 Drinking water suppliers must develop and Assurance 1 Year document standard operating procedures for planned, unplanned and emergency repairs.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=67\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":68,\"text\":\"4.11.3 D3 Facilities Operation, Maintenance and Disinfection Rules Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period D3.12 Drinking water suppliers that have storage Assurance 1 year facilities within a distribution system must prepare a water storage management plan for the operation of storage facilities which includes the minimum and maximum operating levels, target turnover rates, inspection, and cleaning. D3.13 All storage facilities must be subject to an Assurance 1 Year annual security and contamination inspection and assessment by the drinking water supplier. D3.14 Drinking water suppliers must prepare and Assurance 1 Year use written disinfection procedures for storage facilities that are consistent with industry best management practices.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=68\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":68,\"text\":\"D3.15 All new storage facilities, and existing storage Assurance 1 Year facilities that have been drained for maintenance purposes, must be cleaned and disinfected and tested for E. coli prior to being brought (back) into use. D3.16 Divers’ suits, rafts, remotely operated Assurance 1 Year vehicles (ROVs) and other materials used during inspection, maintenance or other activities within storage facility interiors must be made from materials acceptable for contact with drinking water and suitable for disinfection. D3.17 All equipment and materials entering storage Assurance 1 Year facilities must be disinfected immediately prior to entry according to industry best management practices.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=68\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":69,\"text\":\"4.11.4 D3 Residual Disinfection, Disinfection By-product, and Plumbosolvent Metal Rules Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period D3.18 A written sampling plan for determinands to be Assurance 1 Year sampled in relation to disinfection, disinfection by-products and plumbosolvent metals must be prepared including a system map indicating sampling locations and response procedures to be followed when sample results do not meet prescribed levels. D3.19 A FAC of at least 0.2 mg/L must be maintained in Monitoring 1 Month 85% of samples (or 85% of the time if continuously monitored). Up to 15% of samples (or 15% of the time if continuously monitored) may have a FAC of less than 0.2 mg/L but must be greater than 0.1 mg/L. D3.20 Samples must be collected for FAC at the Monitoring 1 Monthly frequencies outlined in table 35.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=69\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":69,\"text\":\"65 D3.21 Routine sampling sites must be located to Assurance 1 Year adequately represent the distribution system and areas associated with higher risk of deterioration in drinking water quality and population exposure. 66 D3.22 Analyses must be undertaken in each Monitoring 1 Year distribution zone according to the frequencies set out in Table 37 for the following disinfection by-products: 1. trihalomethanes: chloroform, bromodichloromethane, dibromochloromethane, and bromoform; and 2. haloacetic acids: dichloroacetic acid and trichloroacetic acid. 65 Demonstrating compliance with this rule (D3.20) is not required if FAC is continuously monitored according to rules D3.25 to D3.27. 66 Demonstrating compliance with this rule (D3.21) is not required if FAC is continuously monitored according to rules D3.25 to D3.27.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=69\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":70,\"text\":\"D3.23 Sample sites for disinfection by-products must Assurance 1 Year represent both peripheral and central locations in the distribution system. D3.24 Analyses must be undertaken in each Monitoring 1 Year distribution zone for the plumbosolvent metals outlined in Table 38. Alternative FAC Continuous Monitoring Rules for Distribution Systems Water suppliers may continuously monitor FAC in a distribution zone in accordance with rules D3.25 to D3.27 as an alternative to demonstrating compliance against rules D3.20 and D3.21. Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period D3.25 Continuous monitoring analysers for FAC must Monitoring 1 Month be installed in each distribution zone at the following locations: 1. At a point where water is provided to a distribution zone, e.g. a reservoir outlet or bulk point of supply. 2.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=70\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":70,\"text\":\"At a supply main within the zone near to the centre or near to the extremity of the distribution zone. D3.26 Continuous monitoring analysers for FAC in Assurance 1 Year each distribution zone must meet the requirements set out in Table 36. D3.27 Confirmation of the representative nature of Assurance 1 Year 67 the continuous monitoring results must be undertaken by the collection and analysis of four FAC and pH grab samples (at least five days between samples) each month from within the distribution zone. Samples should be taken from the outer extent of the distribution zone at times of normal demand. 67 Results from confirmation sampling do not need to be reported or notified for compliance purposes.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=70\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":71,\"text\":\"Table 35. D3 FAC sampling frequency Distribution zone Number of Maximum interval Minimum number population samples per week between samples of days (days) of the week used <25,000 3 4 5 25,001 – 50,000 4 3 6 50,001 – 100,000 5 2 6 (at least two Saturdays and two Sundays sampled each year) >100,000 6 2 7 (at least four Saturdays and four Sundays sampled each year) Table 36. D3 Number of continuous monitoring analysers required in each zone Zone population Number of analysers Up to 25,000 2 25,001 – 100,000 3 >100,000 4\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=71\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":72,\"text\":\"Table 37. D3 Disinfection by-product sampling frequency 68 Disinfection by- products sampling requirements Frequency Each distribution zone. 1 per quarter 69 Table 38. D3 Distribution zone for plumbosolvent metals Plumbosolvent metals Frequency Antimony, cadmium, chromium, copper, lead, mercury, nickel, zinc. Every 6 months 70 68 Additional targeted sampling should be undertaken in accordance with the sampling programme to understand the conditions and circumstances that lead to DBP formation. 69 After 2 years if consecutive samples are less than 50% of the MAV sampling may reduce to 1 per year. If any annual sample exceeds 50% of a MAV, sampling must return to quarterly. 70 Must be sampled monthly if the determinand exceeds 50% of its MAV. Sampling can return to 6 monthly after 12 samples are less than 50% of the MAV.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=72\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":73,\"text\":\"4.11.5 D3 Microbiological Monitoring Rules Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period D3.28 A written sampling plan for monitoring total Assurance 1 Year coliforms, E. coli and any other determinands deemed necessary by the water supplier must be prepared, including a system map indicating sampling locations and a response plan for positive results. D3.29 E. coli and total coliforms must be monitored Reporting 1 Month in each zone of the distribution system according to the frequencies set out in Table 39. D3.30 Routine sampling sites must be located to Assurance 1 Year adequately represent water in the distribution system, including water leaving storage facilities, and entry points for water from another water supplier.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=73\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":73,\"text\":\"D3.31 Samples must be collected according to Assurance 1 Year written sampling protocols prepared by the drinking water supplier or the laboratory undertaking the sample analysis.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=73\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":74,\"text\":\"Table 39. D3 Minimum Microbiological Sampling Frequencies Distribution zone Number of samples Maximum interval Minimum number of population per week between samples days (days) of the week used <25,000 1 9 5 25,001–50,000 1 9 6 (at least two Saturdays and two Sundays sampled each year) 50,001–100,000 2 5 7 (at least three Saturdays and three Sundays sampled each year) >100,001 3 3 7 (at least four Saturdays and four Sundays sampled each year)\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=74\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":75,\"text\":\"4.12 WC Water Carrier Service Rules Module Rule Requirement Assurance/ Compliance Number Monitoring period WC.1 All water to be transported must be sourced Assurance 1 Year from a registered drinking water supply where the water is: 1. safe to drink; and 2. complies with the Drinking Water Standards; and 3. complies with the rules relevant to the supply. WC.2 The water carrier must only take water from a Assurance 1 Year point in a distribution system prescribed by the drinking water supplier. WC.3 If water is sourced from a supply that provides Assurance 1 Year water specifically for water carrier services, that supply must be registered and comply with the rules for Water Carrier Supplies.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=75\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":75,\"text\":\"WC.4 The operator of any vehicle used to transport Assurance 1 Year water must ensure all tanks, and the equipment used for loading or unloading water, are only used for drinking water. WC.5 The operator of any vehicle used to transport Assurance 1 Year water must ensure all tanks, and the equipment used for loading and unloading water, are made from material that light cannot pass through, are kept clean and clear of any possible contaminants at all times, with all openings and connections sealed to protect them from possible contamination. The drinking water must be protected from contamination at all times during its loading, transit and delivery.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=75\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":75,\"text\":\"WC.6 If tanks and the equipment and fittings used for Assurance 1 Year loading and unloading water are not used for the transport of drinking water for a period of 30 or more days, then before next being used to transport drinking water: 1. the tank must be disinfected by filling with drinking water containing at least 5 mg/L FAC for not less than 30 minutes before discharging safely to waste; and 2. equipment and fittings should be washed in water containing 5mg/L FAC.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=75\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":76,\"text\":\"WC.7 The water carrier must ensure there is backflow Assurance 1 Year prevention or an adequate air gap in place when discharging drinking water from their tank. WC.8 When drinking water is delivered, a written Assurance 1 Year statement must be supplied to the customer/consumer stating the: 1. Fill date and time. 2. Registered drinking water supply from which the tanker was loaded. 3. Delivery date, time, location and volume of water delivered. 4. Name and registration number of water carrier. 5. Name and signature of delivery person. The water carrier needs to keep records of this information for a period of 3 years.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=76\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":77,\"text\":\"4.13 TDWS Temporary Drinking Water Supplies Rules (Revoked) Rules revoked on 29 November 2024 Table 40. Removed\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=77\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":78,\"text\":\"4.14 VP Rules for Supplies with Varying Population Module Rule Requirement Rule Type Compliance Number period VP.1 If a supply operating under level 1 treatment or Non-reporting N/A distribution rules exceeds 100 people, the following additional monitoring requirements must be met in the week before the population exceeds 100 people (if predictable) and continuing every week until the population reduces below 100 people: (a) water leaving a treatment plant must be monitored at least weekly for the following: (i) E. coli: (ii) total coliforms: (b) if the supply has distribution zones, water from each distribution zone must be monitored at least weekly for the following: (i) E. coli: (ii) total coliforms.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=78\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":78,\"text\":\"Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 VP.2 If a supply operating under level 1 treatment or level Non-reporting N/A 1 distribution rules exceeds 500 people, the following additional monitoring requirements must be met from 1 week prior before the population exceeds 500 people (if predictable) and continuing every week until the population reduces to below 500 people: (a) water leaving a treatment plant must be monitored at least twice weekly for the following: (i) E. coli: (ii) total coliforms: (b) if the supply has distribution zones, water from each distribution zone must be monitored at least twice weekly for the following: (i) E. coli: (ii) total coliforms: (c) monitoring must continue in accordance with VP.1 where the population reduces below 500 people and remains above 100 people for any period of time.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=78\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":78,\"text\":\"Rule replaced on 29 November 2024\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=78\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":79,\"text\":\"VP.3 If a supply operating under level 2 treatment rules Non-reporting N/A exceeds 500 people, the water leaving the treatment plant must be additionally monitored— (a) at least weekly for the following: (i) E. coli: (ii) total coliforms; and (b) at least daily for the following: (i) turbidity: (ii) FAC (not required for self-supplied buildings): (iii) pH (not required for self-supplied buildings). Rule replaced on 29 November 2024 VP.4 If a supply operating under level 2 distribution rules Non-reporting N/A exceeds 500 people, water in each distribution zone must be additionally monitored— (a) at least weekly for the following: (i) E. coli: (ii) total coliforms; and (b) at least daily from a range of sites across each distribution zone for FAC (not required for self-supplied buildings). Rule inserted on 29 November 2024 Table 41.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=79\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":79,\"text\":\"Removed 71 72 Table 42. Removed 71 Removed 72 Removed\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=79\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":80,\"text\":\"5.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=80\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":80,\"text\":\"Definitions Term Definition abstraction point See section 5 of the Water Services Act 2021. annulus The void between a bore hole and the bore casing. apron (bore) An impervious ground covering, typically concrete, which prevents ingress of surface water into the bore. backflow See section 5 of the Water Services Act 2021. backflow See section 5 of the Water Services Act 2021. prevention device backwash The process of cleaning a filter by reversing a flow of fluid which may contain air through it. barrier A process or infrastructure which prevents or reduces the likelihood of contamination of a drinking water supply. benthic A collective mass of cyanobacteria that forms on the bottom surface of cyanobacterial a water body. mat bore A piped or encased hole constructed to access groundwater. bore head A part of a bore infrastructure located above ground or within the accessible part of an underground access or inspection chamber. building As defined by sections 8 and 9 of the Building Act 2004.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=80\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":80,\"text\":\"C.t value The product of the concentration (C) of a disinfectant and the contact time (t) required to control micro-organisms. calibration Comparing the accuracy of a measuring device to a known standard (which may be a reference device) and adjusting it appropriately. calmed bottom A tank inlet pipe on the floor of a tank which is designed to prevent inlet disturbance of sediment as water enters the tank. cartridge The removable component of a cartridge filter which is used to entrap particulate material. cartridge A treatment which uses a disposable cartridge to remove particulate filtration material from water. certification Written confirmation, usually by a manufacturer, that certain requirements or standards have been met. coagulation A chemical process to neutralise matter in water and which leads to floc formation. consumer See section 5 of the Water Services Act 2021.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=80\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":81,\"text\":\"contact time The hydraulic residence time of a disinfection process.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=81\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":81,\"text\":\"T10 (contact The effective contact time, which is the time it takes 10% of the volume time) of a unit to pass through a tank. cross connection Any actual or potential connection between a drinking water supply and stormwater, wastewater, or other infrastructure. cyanobacteria A major group of micro-organisms capable of photosynthesis, sometimes referred to as blue-green algae. cyanotoxin A toxin produced by cyanobacteria. determinand See the Drinking Water Standards for New Zealand 2022. differential The difference in pressure between two given points, e.g., on the inlet pressure and outlet of a filter. direct filtration A treatment process which consists of coagulation, flocculation, and filtration, without a sedimentation process before filtration. direct integrity A physical test applied to a membrane unit to identify and isolate one test or more leaks that could result in contamination of the filtrate. disinfection The process used to kill or inactivate micro-organisms. disinfection by- A substance formed in drinking water as a result of a disinfection product or DBP process. dissolved air A clarification process in which the flocs formed during coagulation and flotation flocculation are floated to the surface by air bubbles. distribution All components (e.g. buildings, storage tanks, electrical equipment, system reticulation, pumps, instrumentation, etc) of a drinking water supply used to transmit drinking water to consumers or other drinking water suppliers. distribution zone All or part of a distribution system which contains water of a similar character, often defined as a bounded geographic area.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=81\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":81,\"text\":\"Every drinking water supply with a distribution system has at least one distribution zone. domestic self- See section 10 of the Water Services Act 2021. supply / domestic self- supplier drinking water See section 6 of the Water Services Act 2021. Drinking Water See section 49 of the Water Services Act 2021. Quality Assurance Rules (2022) or Rules\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=81\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":82,\"text\":\"Drinking Water See the Water Services (Drinking Water Standards for New Zealand) Standards or Regulations 2022. Also see regulations made under section 47 of the Standards Water Services Act 2021. drinking water See section 8 of the Water Services Act 2021. supplier drinking water As defined by section 9 of the Water Services Act 2021. supply duty (UVI) sensor The duty (on-line) UV sensor installed in a UV reactor that monitors UV intensity during UV equipment operations. Escherichia coli A bacteria species used as an indicator of faecal contamination of or E. coli water.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=82\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":82,\"text\":\"The presence of E. coli almost certainly indicates pathogens harmful to human health are present. event based Monitoring of a drinking water supply to understand the effects of a monitoring specific event on its operation and the safety of drinking water. filtrate Water, other than wash water, leaving a filter. filtration A treatment process that physically removes particles from water by passing it through a medium such as sand or other suitable material. filtration housing The casing containing a cartridge filter or filter material. floating off take A device designed to extract water from the top of water storage infrastructure using a flotation device. flocculation A process that promotes the formation and growth of floc, which are loosely clumped masses of fine particles. free available The chlorine present in chlorinated water in the form of hypochlorous chlorine or FAC acid and hypochlorite ion. free available The free available chlorine equivalent, which is the FAC concentration chlorine that would have the same disinfecting power as the chlorine solution equivalent or would have at pH 8.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=82\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":82,\"text\":\"FACE groundwater Water contained beneath the land surface. inactivate A mechanism which inhibits the reproduction of a micro-organism. instantaneous The amount of recycled water flow being returned to, and entering, the total return rate treatment system at a given point in time. log credit A method for determining a treatment level based on a log-scale control of the target micro-organism. maximum See the Drinking Water Standards 2022. acceptable value or MAV\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=82\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":83,\"text\":\"media filter A filtration process which uses a media, e.g., sand, for the removal of particulate matter. membrane A pressure or vacuum driven separation process in which particulate filtration matter is rejected by an engineered barrier (membrane). microfiltration A filtration process, usually membrane filtration, with pore sizes in the range of 0.1-10 microns (100 - 10,000 nm). nephelometric A unit of measurement for turbidity. turbidity unit (NTU) (oo)cysts Collective term for oocysts and cysts.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=83\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":83,\"text\":\"A Cryptosporidium oocyst is a thick-walled structure within which Cryptosporidium zygotes develop and that serves to transfer the organism to new hosts. operator See section 11 of the Water Services Act 2021. ozone contactor A vessel which facilitates the dissolution of ozone into water and provides sufficient contact time for disinfection. ozone residual Ozone remaining after disinfection has been achieved. pH adjustment The process of changing the pH of water using acidic or basic compounds. planktonic Cyanobacteria which are freely floating in a body of water. cyanobacteria point of supply See section 13 of the Water Services Act 2021. pore size The nominal or absolute size of the holes or apertures in a thin layer of continuous medium, e.g., a membrane filter. property Separate rating unit as defined by the Rating Valuations Act 1998 or a separate rating area under the Local Government (Rating) Act 2002. rapid media filter A media filter which can be pressure or gravity fed and is regularly backwashed. raw water See section 5 of the Water Services Act 2021. recycled water Water, usually discharged from a treatment process which that is returned to the start of the treatment process (i.e., recycled). reduction A dose value for UV systems derived from a UV-dose response curve equivalent dose determined during UV reactor validation. or RED reference (UVI) A calibrated sensor used to check the validation of the duty sensor. sensor registered water See subpart 7 of Part 2 of the Water Services Act 2021. supply\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=83\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":84,\"text\":\"residual A disinfectant, typically chlorine, remaining in the water after it leaves a disinfection treatment plant to act as a barrier to recontamination of water in a distribution system. roof water The rain water collected from the roof off a structure. safe drinking See section 7 of the Water Services Act 2021. water sampling point / A location / tap for the collection of water samples. site sedimentation A treatment process in which solid particles settle to and are removed from the bottom of a clarifier or settling tank. slow sand A filtration process that consists of a bed of fine sand, which is not filtration backwashed and relies on a biologically active layer on top of the sand to remove and degrade particles. slug A disinfection method consisting of placing chlorine liquid in the main (disinfection) to achieve a target concentration when the main is full, completely filling the main to remove all air pockets and flushing the main. source and See section 5 of the Water Services Act 2021. source water source water risk See section 5 of the Water Services Act 2021. management plan spray A process of spraying chlorine solution to disinfect materials, chlorination equipment and fittings used in repairs and construction of water (disinfection) drinking supplies. spring A location where groundwater naturally emerges from the ground surface. surface loading The flow of water through a settling tank or clarifier, divided by the rate effective surface area of the clarifier. surface water A body of water that is open to atmosphere, whether running (streams and rivers) or quiescent (lakes, reservoirs, impoundments and ponds). tablet Disinfection of a pipe using chlorine tablets placed inside the pipe (disinfection) during construction to disinfect the mains once installed. target turnover The ideal amount of time that it takes for the volume of the water in a rate tank to be completely replaced. total coliforms Genera in the family Enterobacteriaceae that will grow on a speciﬁc selective medium when incubated at 35°C ± 0.2°C. turbidity A measure of the suspended particles in a sample that cause loss of clarity by scattering light.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=84\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":85,\"text\":\"ultrafiltration A filtration process, usually membrane filtration, with pore sizes in the range of 0.01-0.1 microns (10 - 100 nm). UV dose The amount of UV radiation emitted into water within a UV reactor calculated by the UV intensity multiplied by the exposure time, usually measured in mJ/cm2. UV intensity or The intensity of UV radiation, usually measured in mW/cm2. UVI UV A measure of the amount of ultraviolet light (typically measured at 254 transmittance or nm) that passes through water.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=85\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Quality Assurance Rules 2022 (Revised 2024)\",\"page\":85,\"text\":\"UVT validation Confirmation that equipment or a process meets, or will meet, certain performance requirements or standards, typically referring to UV reactors or membrane filters verification Checking the accuracy of a measuring device against a known standard (which may be a reference device). waste stream A flow of water from a drinking water treatment plant that is not intended for further treatment or use as drinking water. water carrier See section 5 of the Water Services Act 2021. Water Services See the Water Services Act 2021. Act 2021 or Act\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Drinking-Water-Supplier/Drinking-Water-Quality-Assurance-Rules-2022-Revised-2024.pdf#page=85\",\"kind\":\"rules\"},{\"title\":\"Aesthetic Values Notice 2022\",\"page\":1,\"text\":\"Unclassified Aesthetic Values for Drinking Water Notice 2022 This notice issued pursuant to section 48(1) of the Water Services Act 2021 (Act) by the Chief Executive of Taumata Arowai–the Water Services Regulator (Taumata Arowai), acting under delegated authority, sets out aesthetic values that relate to drinking water. 1. Title This notice is the Aesthetic Values for Drinking Water Notice 2022. 2. Interpretation Within this notice: determinand means a substance or characteristic that is determined or estimated in drinking water. 3. Commencement This notice comes into force on 14 November 2022. 4. Purpose Aesthetic values specify or provide minimum or maximum values for substances and other characteristics that relate to the acceptability of drinking water to consumers (such as appearance, taste, or odour).\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Rules-and-standards/Taumata-Arowai-Aesthetic-Values-for-Drinking-Water-2022.pdf#page=1\",\"kind\":\"aesthetic\"},{\"title\":\"Aesthetic Values Notice 2022\",\"page\":1,\"text\":\"A drinking water supplier must take all reasonably practicable steps to supply drinking water that complies with aesthetic values issued by Taumata Arowai under the Water Services Act 2021. These Aesthetic Values replace the guideline values for aesthetic determinands specified in the Drinking-water Standards for New Zealand 2005 (Revised 2018). 5. Aesthetic values The determinands and corresponding values listed in the Schedule are the aesthetic values that relate to drinking water. Dated at Wellington this 14th day of June 2022 RAYMOND MCMILLAN, Chief executive (Acting)\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Rules-and-standards/Taumata-Arowai-Aesthetic-Values-for-Drinking-Water-2022.pdf#page=1\",\"kind\":\"aesthetic\"},{\"title\":\"Aesthetic Values Notice 2022\",\"page\":2,\"text\":\"Schedule Aesthetic values for drinking water Determinand Value Unit Notes Aluminium ≤ 0.1 mg/L Above this value, complaints of depositions or discoloration may arise Ammonia ≤ 1.5 mg/L Odour threshold (alkaline conditions) Calcium See ‘Hardness’ Chloride ≤ 250 mg/L Taste threshold (counter ion dependent: sodium, calcium or potassium) Chlorine 0.3 ─ 1.0 mg/L as Cl2 Free available chlorine (contingent on the Taste and odour threshold (pH dependant) supply being Disinfection must not be compromised in trying to avoid taste and chlorinated) odour complaints 2-Chlorophenol ≤ 0.0001 mg/L Taste threshold ≤ 0.01 Odour threshold Colour ≤ 15 TCU Appearance Copper ≤1 mg/L Staining of laundry and sanitary ware 1,2-Dichlorobenzene ≤ 0.001 mg/L Taste threshold ≤ 0.002 Odour threshold 1,4-Dichlorobenzene ≤ 0.0003 mg/L Odour threshold ≤ 0.006 Taste threshold 2,4-Dichlorophenol ≤ 0.0003 mg/L Taste threshold ≤ 0.04 Odour threshold Ethylbenzene ≤ 0.002 mg/L Odour threshold ≤ 0.08 Taste threshold Hardness (total) ≤ 200 mg/L Scale deposition, scum formation (pH and alkalinity dependent) (Ca + Mg) as CaCO3 Low hardness (<100) may be more corrosive 100–300 Taste threshold (Ca; counter ion dependent) Hydrogen sulphide ≤ 0.05 mg/L Taste and odour threshold Iron ≤ 0.3 mg/L Staining of laundry and sanitary ware Magnesium See ‘Hardness’ Manganese ≤ 0.04 mg/L Staining of laundry ≤ 0.10 Taste threshold Monochlorobenzene ≤ 0.01 mg/L Taste and odour threshold pH 7.0–8.5 Ideally 7.4 – 8.0.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Rules-and-standards/Taumata-Arowai-Aesthetic-Values-for-Drinking-Water-2022.pdf#page=2\",\"kind\":\"aesthetic\"},{\"title\":\"Aesthetic Values Notice 2022\",\"page\":2,\"text\":\"Most water with a low pH has a high plumbosolvency. Water with a high pH has a soapy taste and feel. A pH less than 8 is preferable for effective disinfection with chlorine\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Rules-and-standards/Taumata-Arowai-Aesthetic-Values-for-Drinking-Water-2022.pdf#page=2\",\"kind\":\"aesthetic\"},{\"title\":\"Aesthetic Values Notice 2022\",\"page\":3,\"text\":\"Sodium ≤ 200 mg/L Taste threshold (counter ion dependent) Styrene ≤ 0.004 mg/L Odour threshold Sulphate ≤ 250 mg/L Taste threshold Taste and odour Acceptable to most consumers Temperature ≤ 15°C Toluene ≤ 0.03 mg/L Odour threshold ≤ 0.04 Taste threshold Total dissolved solids ≤ 1000 mg/L Taste may become unacceptable from 600–1200 mg/L Trichlorobenzenes See below (total) 1,2,3-Trichlorobenzene ≤ 0.01 mg/L Odour threshold 1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene ≤ 0.005 mg/L Odour threshold 1,3,5-Trichlorobenzene ≤ 0.05 mg/L Odour threshold 2,4,6-Trichlorophenol ≤ 0.002 mg/L Taste threshold ≤ 0.3 mg/L Odour threshold Turbidity ≤5 NTU Appearance Xylene ≤ 0.02 mg/L Odour threshold Zinc ≤ 1.5 mg/L Taste threshold\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Rules-and-standards/Taumata-Arowai-Aesthetic-Values-for-Drinking-Water-2022.pdf#page=3\",\"kind\":\"aesthetic\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":1,\"text\":\"This legislation is administered by the Water Services Authority—Taumata Arowai. For more information please see: Website: http://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz Contact phone: +64 4 889 8350 Contact address: PO Box 628, Wellington 6140, New Zealand Water Services (Small to Medium Networks) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025 This Drinking Water Acceptable Solution is made under section 50 of the Water Services Act 2021 by the Chief Executive of the Water Services Authority—Taumata Arowai acting under delegated authority after consultation in accordance with section 53 of that Act.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=1\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":1,\"text\":\"Contents Page 1 Title 1 2 Commencement 1 3 Interpretation 1 4 Meaning of small to medium networked drinking water supply 3 5 Application 3 6 Compliance with the Water Services Act 2021 3 7 Revocation and transition 3 Schedule 1 4 Requirements for Small to Medium Networks 1 Title This is the Water Services (Small to Medium Networks) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025. 2 Commencement This Drinking Water Acceptable Solution comes into force on 5 September 2025. 3 Interpretation In this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution, unless the context otherwise requires,—\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=1\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":2,\"text\":\"Act means the Water Services Act 2021 base population means the number of people that are normally supplied drinking water at a connection using an end-point treatment system regardless of any seasonal or temporary increases bore means a piped or encased hole constructed to access groundwater building means a stand-alone structure where drinking water is provided.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=2\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":2,\"text\":\"A building does not include a structure where only non-potable water is provided for other uses consumer’s property means one property, or two or more properties with common ownership arrangements, that receives drinking water from a drinking water supply and is not owned or controlled by the drinking water supplier designated validation standard means one of the following: (a) NSF/ANSI 55 Class A: (b) USEPA Ultraviolet Disinfection Guidance Manual: (c) DVGW Technical Standard W294-1:2023-12: (d) ÖNORM M 5873-1: 2020 01 01: (e) DIN 19294-1:2020-08 determinand means a substance or characteristic that is determined or estimated in drinking water distribution system means all components of a drinking water supply used to transmit drinking water to consumers’ properties or other drinking water supplies including buildings, storage tanks, electrical equipment, pipes and pumps end-point treatment system means the combined system of end-point treatment devices at the point of supply IANZ means International Accreditation New Zealand, a trading entity of the Accreditation Council continued under the Standards and Accreditation Act 2015 MAV means the maximum acceptable value of a determinand as set out in the Water Services (Drinking Water Standards for New Zealand) Regulations 2022 network means a distribution system that provides drinking water to consumers’ properties or other drinking water supplies.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=2\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":2,\"text\":\"A network may provide water at a pressure and volume to meet consumer demand, or at a restricted flow and volume pre-treatment means infrastructure or processes to treat drinking water to improve water quality prior to distribution to connected properties that use end-point treatment roof water means the rainwater collected from the roof of a building or structure small to medium networked drinking water supply has the meaning set out in clause 4 of this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution spring means a location where groundwater naturally emerges from the ground surface surface water means a lake, river, stream or similar body of water that is located on top of land UV means ultraviolet light UV transmittance means the measurement of the amount of UV light that can pass through water usually measured as a percentage 2\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=2\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":3,\"text\":\"4 Meaning of small to medium networked drinking water supply (1) In this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution, small to medium networked drinking water supply means a drinking water supply that provides drinking water via a network primarily to consumers’ properties and may also provide drinking water to another drinking water supply. (2) A small to medium drinking networked drinking water supply must serve no more than 500 people (additional population limits for buildings served by end-point treatment apply). 5 Application (1) This Drinking Water Acceptable Solution may be used only by drinking water suppliers who own or operate small to medium networked drinking water supplies. (2) Bore, spring, roof or surface water may be used as source water for the drinking water supply.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=3\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":3,\"text\":\"(3) A consumer may supplement the drinking water supply with drinking water from another source (for example, self-supplied roof water) or from another drinking water supply (for example, a registered water carrier).\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=3\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":3,\"text\":\"6 Compliance with the Water Services Act 2021 (1) Drinking water suppliers who ensure that a small to medium networked drinking water supply meets all the applicable requirements in Schedule 1 are deemed to comply with the following sections of the Act: (a) section 24 – duty to take reasonable steps to supply aesthetically acceptable drinking water: (b) section 27 – duty to protect against risk of backflow: (c) section 30 – owner must have a drinking water safety plan: (d) section 37(1) – drinking water suppliers to keep records: (e) section 43 – source water risk management plans: (f) section 49(3) – duty to comply with all applicable compliance rules.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=3\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":3,\"text\":\"(2) For the avoidance of doubt, drinking water suppliers who comply with the requirements in Schedule 1 are not required to prepare a drinking water safety plan (including a source water risk management plan) or demonstrate compliance with compliance rules (for example, the Drinking Water Quality Assurance Rules). 7 Revocation and transition (1) The Drinking Water Acceptable Solution for Spring and Bore Water Supplies 2022 and the Drinking Water Acceptable Solution for Roof Water Supplies 2022 are revoked from the date of commencement of this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=3\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":3,\"text\":\"(2) Drinking water suppliers that were using the Drinking Water Acceptable Solution for Spring and Bore Water Supplies 2022 or the Drinking Water Acceptable Solution for Roof Water Supplies 2022 at the date of commencement of this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution— (a) are deemed to be compliant with this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution for 1 year from that date if the previous requirements continue to be met; or (b) may transition to this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution at any time following commencement. 3\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=3\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":4,\"text\":\"Schedule 1 Requirements for Small to Medium Networks Contents Page End-point treatment SMN.1 End-point treatment must be used at consumers’ properties 5 SMN.2 Exceptions to end-point treatment requirements may apply in respect of connected drinking water supplies 5 SMN.3 End-point treatment must meet population and building limits 5 SMN.4 End-point treatment must be effective 5 SMN.5 UV disinfection systems must be validated for designated buildings 6 Water quality SMN.6 Water must be suitable for end-point treatment 6 SMN.7 Pre-treatment may be used 7 SMN.8 Bore, spring and surface water must be monitored 7 SMN.9 Cyanobacteria/cyanotoxin risk must be identified and managed for surface water sources 8 SMN.10 Springs and bores must be protected 8 SMN.11 Roof water must be monitored 9 SMN.12 Risks to roof water quality must be minimised 9 Information provision SMN.13 Monitoring results must be made available 9 SMN.14 Information about testing must be provided 10 SMN.15 Information on how to minimise risks associated with consumers’ storage tanks must be provided 10 SMN.16 Information must be provided to owners of premises who are required to install, maintain and test end-point treatment devices 10 Backflow prevention SMN.17 All connections must have backflow prevention 10 Emergency management, record-keeping and capability SMN.18 Events and emergencies must be managed appropriately 11 SMN.19 Records of the drinking water supply must be kept 11 SMN.20 People working on the drinking water supply must be competent 11 4\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=4\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":5,\"text\":\"End-point treatment SMN.1 End-point treatment must be used at consumers’ properties End-point treatment that meets the requirements of SMN.3 to SMN.5 must be installed and maintained at consumers’ properties for any buildings where the water supplied is used as drinking water unless SMN.2 applies.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=5\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":5,\"text\":\"SMN.2 Exceptions to end-point treatment requirements may apply in respect of connected drinking water supplies Requirements relating to end-point treatment are not required to be met in respect of any point of supply where— (a) drinking water is supplied from a small to medium network (Supply A) to another drinking water supply (Supply B); and (b) a general exemption under section 57 of the Act exempts Supplier A from the duties to supply safe drinking water and comply with drinking water standards in respect of drinking water supplied to Supplier B.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=5\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":5,\"text\":\"SMN.3 End-point treatment must meet population and building limits (a) Each end-point treatment system must either— (i) provide drinking water to a single building where the base population does not exceed 500 people; or (ii) provide drinking water to no more than three buildings on one property or adjoining properties with common ownership arrangements where the base population does not exceed 100 people. (b) The population supplied by each end-point treatment system may exceed its base population limit if— (i) the exceedance is for a total of no more than 60 days in any 12- month period; and (ii) the end-point treatment system has the capacity to effectively treat drinking water for these periods.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=5\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":5,\"text\":\"SMN.4 End-point treatment must be effective The following requirements must be met to ensure that all end-point treatment systems are effective for treating drinking water: (a) an end-point treatment system must consist of at a least a cartridge filtration system and a UV disinfection system: (b) each component of an end-point treatment system must be installed— (i) to meet the peak instantaneous demand for treated drinking water at the connected property; and (ii) in accordance with the manufacturer’s instructions and requirements: 5\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=5\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":6,\"text\":\"(c) an end-point treatment system must include at least one cartridge filter, installed before the UV disinfection system and with the final or only cartridge having a 5 micron or less nominal pore size: (d) every device in the end-point treatment system must be operated and maintained in accordance with the manufacturer’s instructions: (e) the UV disinfection system must deliver at least 40 mJ/cm2 reduction equivalent dose of UV light: (f) water flowing through the UV disinfection system must be restricted or monitored so that the flow rate does not exceed the flow rate specified by the manufacturer for 40 mJ/cm2 reduction equivalent dose of UV light: (g) lamp usage in the UV disinfection system must not exceed the manufacturer's recommendations: (h) written evidence must be available (for example, the manufacturer’s website, or the instruction manual) confirming— (i) the UV disinfection system delivers at least 40 mJ/cm2 reduction equivalent dose of UV light; and (ii) the flow rate specified by the manufacturer for 40 mJ/cm2 reduction equivalent dose of UV light.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=6\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":6,\"text\":\"SMN.5 UV disinfection systems must be validated for designated buildings An end-point treatment UV disinfection system must be validated to and operate within the specifications of a designated validation standard, for any buildings— (a) with a base population that exceeds 25 people; or (b) with a base population of any size; if— (i) the building has a community purpose (for example, a school, marae, sports club, or community hall); or (ii) the building has drinking water available to the public (for example a cafe, hotel, or camping ground); but (c) notwithstanding (a) and (b), validation is not required where a UV disinfection system was installed before 17 October 2022 and written evidence is available (for example, the manufacturer’s website, or the instruction manual) that the unit delivers at least 40 mJ/cm2 reduction equivalent dose of UV light.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=6\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":6,\"text\":\"Water quality SMN.6 Water must be suitable for end-point treatment Where end-point treatment is used, all water in the distribution system must meet the following requirements to be suitable for cartridge filtration and UV disinfection: (a) turbidity must be low enough to ensure cartridge filters will last for at least one month before replacement of cartridges is required: 6\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=6\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":7,\"text\":\"(b) UV transmittance must be high enough for the UV disinfection system to achieve at least 40 mJ/cm2 reduction equivalent dose of UV light: (c) iron must be low enough to ensure the UV disinfection system sleeve is not fouled with iron deposits within one year before cleaning is required: (d) manganese must be low enough to ensure the UV disinfection system sleeve is not fouled with manganese deposits within one year before cleaning is required: (e) chemical determinands that are required to be monitored under SMN.8 and SMN.11 must not exceed their respective MAV in the drinking water standards: (f) where surface water is used, cyanobacteria/cyanotoxin risk in must be either assessed as low or managed to mitigate the risk prior to end-point treatment.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=7\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":7,\"text\":\"SMN.7 Pre-treatment may be used (a) Pre-treatment may be used to partially treat drinking water to meet the requirements of SMN.6 prior to end-point treatment. (b) Chlorine may be added to a drinking water supply to control bacterial and viral pathogens and to reduce the risk of growth of biofilms, however it is not required to meet this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution. SMN.8 Bore, spring and surface water must be monitored (a) Where the source water is or includes bore, spring or surface water, the following determinands must be monitored in the supply distribution system, following any central pre-treatment and prior to end-point treatment, before this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution is used and then— (i) at least every three months for the following microbiological determinands: a.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=7\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":7,\"text\":\"E. coli: b. total coliforms; and (ii) at least every three months for the following physical determinands: a. turbidity: b. UV transmittance; and (iii) at least annually for the following chemical determinands: a. iron: b. manganese: c. nitrate: d. arsenic: e. boron: f. hardness: 7\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=7\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":8,\"text\":\"g. any other chemical determinands that are identified as presenting a risk to the supply. (b) Microbiological and chemical samples collected for SMN.8(a) must be— (i) collected according to any instructions and specifications provided by the laboratory; and (ii) analysed by a laboratory accredited by IANZ for the type of analysis being undertaken.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=8\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":8,\"text\":\"SMN.9 Cyanobacteria/cyanotoxin risk must be identified and managed for surface water sources Where the source water is or includes surface water, the following requirements must be met: (a) monitoring or visual inspections of the water and area around and upstream of any surface water intake must be undertaken each month between October and May (inclusive) for the presence of benthic cyanobacteria mats and planktonic cyanobacterial growth: (b) if there is evidence of cyanobacterial growth steps must be taken to assess the cyanotoxin risk to consumers: (c) if there is a risk of supplying water with cyanotoxins that exceed a MAV, a response plan must be developed and followed under SMN.18: (d) consumer taste or odour complaints, which have the potential to relate to cyanobacteria, must be— (i) recorded; and (ii) investigated to determine the cause.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=8\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":8,\"text\":\"SMN.10 Springs and bores must be protected Where the source water is or includes a spring or a bore, the following requirements must be met: (a) springs and bores must be protected by headworks which minimise the risk of contamination from nearby surface water: (b) farm animals must be excluded from an area extending at least five metres in all directions from the headworks of a bore, spring or spring-fed pond: (c) springs and bores must not be affected by contamination from any of the following: (i) a sewage disposal field or effluent discharge (for example, a septic tank or other wastewater treatment system): (ii) an underground storage tank (such as at a petrol station): (iii) a waste pond: (iv) a landfill: (v) an offal pit: (vi) areas where pesticides or animal effluent is applied to land: 8\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=8\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":9,\"text\":\"(vii) aquifers contaminated with, or at risk of contamination with, sewage from exfiltration and/or pump station overflows: (viii) contaminated sites: (d) springs and bores must not provide geothermal water. SMN.11 Roof water must be monitored (a) Where the source water is or includes roof water, the following determinands must be monitored in untreated roof water before this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution is used and then— (i) at least every three months for the following microbiological determinands: a.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=9\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":9,\"text\":\"E. coli: b. total coliforms; and (ii) at least every three years for the following chemical determinands: a. cadmium: b. copper; and c. lead (b) Microbiological and chemical samples collected for SMN.11(a) must be— (i) collected according to any instructions and specifications provided by the laboratory; and (ii) analysed by a laboratory accredited by IANZ for the type of analysis being undertaken.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=9\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":9,\"text\":\"SMN.12 Risks to roof water quality must be minimised Where the source water is or includes roof water, the following requirements must be met: (a) as far as practicable, roof surfaces used for the collection of drinking water must be kept free from decaying debris, leaves, branches, bird nests and faeces from animals or birds: (b) the roof supply must be able to be disconnected from the water storage and treatment system for cleaning and maintenance. Information provision SMN.13 Monitoring results must be made available The results of all water quality monitoring required under SMN.8 and SMN.11 must be made available to consumers and other drinking water suppliers within a reasonable time after receiving the results. 9\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=9\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":10,\"text\":\"SMN.14 Information about testing must be provided The following testing information must be provided to consumers at least annually and when the drinking water supplier becomes aware that property ownership has changed: (a) a recommendation to test drinking water treated by end-point systems for E. coli and total coliforms at least every 6 months: (b) where to find a laboratory accredited by IANZ: (c) advice to increase testing to weekly for any periods when the base population temporarily increases following SMN.3(b).\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=10\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":10,\"text\":\"SMN.15 Information on how to minimise risks associated with consumers’ storage tanks must be provided The following recommendations must be provided to consumers at least annually and when the supplier becomes aware that property ownership has changed: (a) treated drinking water storage tanks should be secured against the ingress of rainwater and surface water: (b) inlets, lids, overflows and any other small gaps in treated and untreated drinking water storage tanks should be secure from contamination by vermin, birds, animals, faecal material, or other material: (c) any treated or untreated drinking water storage tanks should be inspected annually to ensure the above are met: (d) untreated water from any other source should be first delivered into an untreated water storage tank so that all water to be used for drinking passes through the end-point treatment system.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=10\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":10,\"text\":\"SMN.16 Information must be provided to owners of premises who are required to install, maintain and test end-point treatment devices If the drinking water supplier requires the owner of the premises to install, maintain, and test an end-point treatment device under section 28(3)(b) of the Act, information must be provided in writing at least annually, and when the supplier becomes aware that property ownership has changed, that advises the owner of— (a) the minimum requirements for the end-point treatment device that must be installed to meet this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution; and (b) the maintenance requirements for each end-point treatment device, (for example changing UV lamps and cartridge filters); and (c) testing or monitoring requirements to verify that the end-point treatment device is operating correctly.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=10\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":10,\"text\":\"Backflow prevention SMN.17 All connections must have backflow prevention (a) Backflow prevention must be installed on all connections to the drinking water supply regardless of whether the water is used for drinking after that connection. 10\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=10\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":11,\"text\":\"(b) Backflow prevention must be one of the following types: (i) air gaps: (ii) non-testable dual check valves: (iii) non-testable double check valves: (iv) testable backflow prevention devices. (c) Air gaps used for backflow prevention must be inspected annually. (d) Testable backflow prevention devices must— (i) be installed if there is a medium or high backflow risk; and (ii) be inspected and tested annually. Emergency management, record-keeping and capability SMN.18 Events and emergencies must be managed appropriately (a) Events and emergencies that could compromise the ability of end-point treatment systems to make drinking water safe or put the supply of a sufficient quantity of drinking water at risk must be identified and documented.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=11\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":11,\"text\":\"(b) A response plan must be developed and followed for each event or emergency identified under SMN.18(a). SMN.19 Records of the drinking water supply must be kept The following records must be kept and maintained for at least three years: (a) records of installation, maintenance and testing of end-point treatment devices: (b) records of inspection and maintenance of supply components including storage tanks, pipes and pumps: (c) records of all monitoring undertaken by the drinking water supplier: (d) records of all information provided to consumers: (e) records of installation, inspection and testing of backflow prevention: (f) records of response plans and responses to events and emergencies.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=11\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":11,\"text\":\"SMN.20 People working on the drinking water supply must be competent People who install, maintain or operate any aspect of the drinking water supply must be suitably qualified, trained or experienced to undertake the relevant task. Made at Wellington on 3 September 2025. Allan Prangnell Chief Executive Water Services Authority—Taumata Arowai 11\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=11\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":12,\"text\":\"Explanatory note This note is not part of the drinking water acceptable solution but is intended to indicate its general effect. This drinking water acceptable solution, which comes into force on 5 September 2025, provides a prescribed set of requirements that small to medium networked drinking water suppliers can use to establish compliance with particular duties in the Act. The population limit for the drinking water supply is 500 people. End-point treatment systems consisting of cartridge filtration and UV disinfection are required at consumers’ properties as an alternative to treating drinking water centrally. An end-point treatment system can serve one building with no more than 500 people or up to 3 buildings with no more than 100 people.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=12\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":12,\"text\":\"Validated UV systems are required for buildings with over 25 people or buildings that have a community purpose or are open to the public. Drinking water supplied must be of a quality suitable for end-point treatment, key determinands must be monitored, spring and bore sources must be protected, information must be provided to consumers, backflow prevention must be installed, emergencies must be managed, records must be kept and people working on the supply must be competent. A drinking water supplier who meets the criteria and requirements is not required to comply with compliance rules made under the Act and prepare a drinking water safety plan.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=12\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":12,\"text\":\"The Drinking Water Acceptable Solution for Spring and Bore Water Supplies 2022 and the Drinking Water Acceptable Solution for Roof Water Supplies 2022 are revoked and replaced with separate acceptable solutions for small to medium networks and self-supplied buildings. This is secondary legislation issued under the authority of the Legislation Act 2019.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=12\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Small to Medium Networks Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":12,\"text\":\"Title Water Services (Small to Medium Networks) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025 Principal or amendment Principal Consolidated version No Empowering Act and provisions Water Services Act 2021 Section 50 Replacement empowering Act and provisions Maker name Water Services Authority—Taumata Arowai Administering agency Water Services Authority—Taumata Arowai Date made 3 September 2025 Publication date 4 September 2025 Notification date 4 September 2025 Commencement date 5 September 2025 End date (when applicable) Consolidation as at date Not applicable Related instruments 12\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Small-to-Medium-Networks-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=12\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":1,\"text\":\"This legislation is administered by the Water Services Authority—Taumata Arowai. For more information please see: Website: http://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz Contact phone: +64 4 889 8350 Contact address: PO Box 628, Wellington 6140, New Zealand Water Services (Mixed-use Rural) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025 This Drinking Water Acceptable Solution is made under section 50 of the Water Services Act 2021 by the Chief Executive of the Water Services Authority—Taumata Arowai acting under delegated authority after consultation in accordance with section 53 of that Act.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=1\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":1,\"text\":\"Contents Page 1 Title 1 2 Commencement 1 3 Interpretation 1 4 Meaning of mixed-use rural water scheme 3 5 Application 3 6 Compliance with the Water Services Act 2021 3 7 Revocation and transition 3 Schedule 1 4 Requirements for Mixed-use Rural Water Schemes 1 Title This is the Water Services (Mixed-use Rural) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025. 2 Commencement This Drinking Water Acceptable Solution comes into force on 5 September 2025. 3 Interpretation In this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution, unless the context otherwise requires,—\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=1\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":2,\"text\":\"Act means the Water Services Act 2021 base population means the number of people that are normally supplied drinking water at a connection using an end-point treatment system regardless of any seasonal or temporary increases bore means a piped or encased hole constructed to access groundwater building means a stand-alone structure where drinking water is provided.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=2\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":2,\"text\":\"A building does not include a structure where only non-potable water is provided for other uses consumer’s property means one property, or two or more properties with common ownership arrangements, that receives drinking water from a mixed-use rural water scheme and is not owned or controlled by the drinking water supplier designated validation standard means one of the following: (a) NSF/ANSI 55 Class A: (b) USEPA Ultraviolet Disinfection Guidance Manual: (c) DVGW Technical Standard W294-1:2023-12: (d) ÖNORM M 5873-1: 2020 01 01: (e) DIN 19294-1:2020-08 determinand means a substance or characteristic that is determined or estimated in drinking water distribution system means all components of a drinking water supply used to transmit drinking water to consumers’ properties or other drinking water supplies including buildings, storage tanks, electrical equipment, pipes and pumps end-point treatment system means the combined system of end-point treatment devices at the point of supply IANZ means International Accreditation New Zealand, a trading entity of the Accreditation Council continued under the Standards and Accreditation Act 2015 MAV means the maximum acceptable value of a determinand as set out in the Water Services (Drinking Water Standards for New Zealand) Regulations 2022 mixed-use rural water scheme has the meaning set out in clause 4 of this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution network means distribution system that provides drinking water to consumers’ properties or other drinking water supplies.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=2\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":2,\"text\":\"A network may provide water at a pressure and volume to meet consumer demand, or at a restricted flow and volume pre-treatment means infrastructure or processes to treat drinking water to improve water quality prior to distribution to connected properties that use end-point treatment spring means a location where groundwater naturally emerges from the ground surface surface water means a lake, river, stream or similar body of water that is located on top of land UV means ultraviolet light UV transmittance means the measurement of the amount of UV light that can pass through water, usually measured as a percentage 2\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=2\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":3,\"text\":\"4 Meaning of mixed-use rural water scheme In this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution, mixed-used rural water scheme means a drinking water supply that provides drinking water via a network to consumers’ properties or to other drinking water supplies where the following statements are true: (a) no less than 50% of the water supplied is intended to be used for agricultural purposes or horticultural purposes, or both: (b) no more than 50% of the water supplied is intended to be used for domestic purposes (including for drinking water). 5 Application (1) This Drinking Water Acceptable Solution may be used only by drinking water suppliers who own or operate mixed-use rural water schemes. (2) There is no maximum population limit for the drinking water supply (population limits apply to buildings served by end-point treatment systems).\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=3\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":3,\"text\":\"(3) Bore, spring or surface water may be used as source water for the drinking water supply. (4) A drinking water supplier is not required to meet any treatment requirements for any connected property where water is only used for purposes other than drinking water. (5) A consumer may supplement the drinking water supply from the mixed-use rural water scheme with drinking water from other sources (for example, self-supplied roof water) or from other drinking water supplies (for example, a registered water carrier).\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=3\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":3,\"text\":\"6 Compliance with the Water Services Act 2021 (1) Drinking water suppliers who ensure that a mixed-use rural water scheme meets all the applicable requirements in Schedule 1 are deemed to comply with the following sections of the Act: (a) section 24 – duty to take reasonable steps to supply aesthetically acceptable drinking water: (b) section 27 – duty to protect against risk of backflow: (c) section 30 – owner must have a drinking water safety plan: (d) section 37(1) – drinking water suppliers to keep records: (e) section 43 – source water risk management plans: (f) section 49(3) – duty to comply with all applicable compliance rules.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=3\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":3,\"text\":\"(2) For the avoidance of doubt, drinking water suppliers who comply with the requirements in Schedule 1 are not required to prepare a drinking water safety plan (including a source water risk management plan) or demonstrate compliance with compliance rules (for example, the Drinking Water Quality Assurance Rules). 7 Revocation and transition (1) The Drinking Water Acceptable Solution for Mixed-use Rural Water Supplies 2022 is revoked from the date of commencement of this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=3\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":3,\"text\":\"(2) Drinking water suppliers that were using the Drinking Water Acceptable Solution for Mixed-use Rural Water Supplies 2022 at the date of commencement of this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution— (a) are deemed to be compliant with this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution for 1 year from that date if the previous requirements continue to be met; or (b) may transition to this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution at any time following commencement. 3\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=3\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":4,\"text\":\"Schedule 1 Requirements for Mixed-use Rural Water Schemes Contents Page End-point treatment MUR.1 End-point treatment must be used at consumers’ properties 5 MUR.2 Exceptions to end-point treatment requirements may apply in respect of connected drinking water supplies 5 MUR.3 End-point treatment must meet population and building limits 5 MUR.4 End-point treatment must be effective 5 MUR.5 UV disinfection systems must be validated for designated buildings 6 Water quality MUR.6 Water must be suitable for end-point treatment 6 MUR.7 Pre-treatment may be used 7 MUR.8 Water in the distribution system must be monitored 7 MUR.9 Cyanobacteria/cyanotoxin risk must be identified and managed for surface water 8 MUR.10 Springs and bores must be protected 8 Information provision MUR.11 Monitoring results must be made available 9 MUR.12 Information about testing must be provided 9 MUR.13 Information on how to minimise risks associated with consumers’ storage tanks must be provided 9 MUR.14 Information must be provided to owners of premises who are required to install, maintain and test end-point treatment devices 9 Backflow prevention MUR.15 All connections must have backflow prevention 10 Emergency management, record-keeping and capability MUR.16 Events and emergencies must be identified and managed appropriately 10 MUR.17 Records of the drinking water supply must be kept 10 MUR.18 People working on a drinking water supply must be competent 10 4\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=4\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":5,\"text\":\"End-point treatment MUR.1 End-point treatment must be used at consumers’ properties End-point treatment that meets the requirements of MUR.3 to MUR.5 must be installed and maintained at consumers’ properties for any buildings where the water supplied is used as drinking water unless MUR.2 applies.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=5\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":5,\"text\":\"MUR.2 Exceptions to end-point treatment requirements may apply in respect of connected drinking water supplies Requirements relating to end-point treatment are not required to be met in respect of any point of supply where— (a) drinking water is supplied from a mixed-use rural scheme (Supply A) to another drinking water supply (Supply B); and (b) a general exemption under section 57 of the Act exempts the Supplier A from the duties to supply safe drinking water and comply with drinking water standards in respect of drinking water supplied to Supplier B.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=5\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":5,\"text\":\"MUR.3 End-point treatment must meet population and building limits (a) Each end-point treatment system must either— (i) provide drinking water to a single building where the base population does not exceed 500 people; or (ii) provide drinking water to no more than three buildings on one property, or on adjoining properties with common ownership arrangements, where the base population does not exceed 100 people. (b) The population supplied by each end-point treatment system may exceed its base population limit if— (i) the exceedance is for a total of no more than 60 days in any 12- month period; and (ii) the end-point treatment system has the capacity to effectively treat drinking water for these periods.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=5\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":5,\"text\":\"MUR.4 End-point treatment must be effective The following requirements must be met to ensure that all end-point treatment systems are effective for treating drinking water: (a) an end-point treatment system must consist of at a least a cartridge filtration system and a UV disinfection system: (b) each component of an end-point treatment system must be installed— (i) to meet the peak instantaneous demand for treated drinking water at the connected property; and (ii) in accordance with the manufacturer’s instructions and requirements: 5\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=5\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":6,\"text\":\"(c) an end-point treatment system must include at least one cartridge filter, installed before the UV disinfection system and with the final or only cartridge having a 5 micron or less nominal pore size: (d) every device in an end-point treatment system must be operated and maintained in accordance with the manufacturer’s instructions: (e) the UV disinfection system must deliver at least 40 mJ/cm2 reduction equivalent dose of UV light: (f) water flowing through the UV disinfection system must be restricted or monitored so that the flow rate does not exceed the flow rate specified by the manufacturer for 40 mJ/cm2 reduction equivalent dose of UV light: (g) lamp usage in the UV disinfection system must not exceed the manufacturer's recommendations: (h) written evidence must be available (for example, from the manufacturer’s website, or the instruction manual) confirming— (i) the UV disinfection system delivers at least 40 mJ/cm2 reduction equivalent dose of UV light; and (ii) the flow rate specified by the manufacturer for 40 mJ/cm2 reduction equivalent dose of UV light.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=6\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":6,\"text\":\"MUR.5 UV disinfection systems must be validated for designated buildings An end-point treatment UV disinfection system must be validated to and operate within the specifications of a designated validation standard for any buildings— (a) with a base population that exceeds 25 people; or (b) with a base population of any size; if— (i) the building has a community purpose (for example, a school, marae, sports club, or community hall); or (ii) the building has drinking water available to the public (for example a cafe, hotel, or camping ground); but (c) notwithstanding (a) and (b), validation is not required where a UV disinfection system was installed before 17 October 2022 and written evidence is available (for example, the manufacturer’s website, or the instruction manual) that the unit delivers at least 40 mJ/cm2 reduction equivalent dose of UV light.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=6\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":6,\"text\":\"Water quality MUR.6 Water must be suitable for end-point treatment Where end-point treatment is used, all water in the distribution system must meet the following requirements to be suitable for cartridge filtration and UV disinfection: (a) turbidity must be low enough to ensure cartridge filters will last for at least one month before replacement of cartridges is required: 6\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=6\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":7,\"text\":\"(b) UV transmittance must be high enough for the UV disinfection system to achieve at least 40 mJ/cm2 reduction equivalent dose of UV light: (c) iron must be low enough to ensure the UV disinfection system sleeve is not fouled with iron deposits within one year before cleaning is required: (d) manganese must be low enough to ensure the UV disinfection system sleeve is not fouled with manganese deposits within one year before cleaning is required: (e) chemical determinands that are required to be monitored under MUR.8 must not exceed their respective MAV in the drinking water standards: (f) where surface water is used, cyanobacteria/cyanotoxin risk must be either assessed as low or managed to mitigate the risk prior to end point treatment.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=7\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":7,\"text\":\"MUR.7 Pre-treatment may be used (a) Pre-treatment may be used to partially treat drinking water to meet the requirements of MUR.6 prior to end-point treatment. (b) Chlorine may be added to a drinking water supply to control bacterial and viral pathogens and to reduce the risk of growth of biofilms, however it is not required to meet this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution. MUR.8 Water in the distribution system must be monitored (a) The following determinands must be monitored in the supply distribution system, following any central pre-treatment and prior to end-point treatment, before this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution is used and then— (i) at least every three months for microbiological determinands: a. E. coli: b. total coliforms; and (ii) at least every three months for physical determinands: a. turbidity: b.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=7\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":7,\"text\":\"UV transmittance; and (iii) at least annually for the following chemical determinands: a. iron: b. manganese: c. nitrate: d. arsenic: e. boron: f. hardness: g. any other chemical determinands that are identified as presenting a risk to the supply. 7\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=7\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":8,\"text\":\"(b) Microbiological and chemical and samples collected for MUR.8(a) must be— (i) collected according to any instructions and specifications provided by the laboratory; and (ii) analysed by a laboratory accredited by IANZ for the type of analysis being undertaken.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=8\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":8,\"text\":\"MUR.9 Cyanobacteria/cyanotoxin risk must be identified and managed for surface water Where the source water is or includes surface water, the following requirements must be met: (a) monitoring or visual inspections of the water and area around and upstream of any surface water intake must be undertaken each month between October and May (inclusive) for the presence of benthic cyanobacteria mats and planktonic cyanobacterial growth: (b) if there is evidence of cyanobacterial growth steps must be taken to assess the cyanotoxin risk to consumers: (c) if there is a risk of supplying water with cyanotoxins that exceed a MAV, a response plan must be developed and followed under MUR.16: (d) consumer taste or odour complaints, which have the potential to relate to cyanobacteria, must be— (i) recorded; and (ii) investigated to determine the cause.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=8\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":8,\"text\":\"MUR.10 Springs and bores must be protected Where the source water is or includes a spring or a bore, the following requirements must be met: (a) springs and bores must be protected by headworks which minimise the risk of contamination from nearby surface water: (b) farm animals must be excluded from an area extending at least five metres in all directions from the headworks of a bore, spring or spring-fed pond: (c) springs and bores must not be affected by contamination from any of the following: (i) a sewage disposal field or effluent discharge (for example, a septic tank or other wastewater treatment system): (ii) an underground storage tank (such as at a petrol station): (iii) a waste pond: (iv) a landfill: (v) an offal pit: (vi) areas where pesticides or animal effluent is applied to land: (vii) aquifers contaminated with, or at risk of contamination with, sewage from exfiltration and/or pump station overflows: (viii) contaminated sites: (d) springs and bores must not provide geothermal water.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=8\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":8,\"text\":\"8\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=8\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":9,\"text\":\"Information provision MUR.11 Monitoring results must be made available The results of all water quality monitoring required under MUR.8 must be made available to consumers and other drinking water suppliers within a reasonable time after receiving the results. MUR.12 Information about testing must be provided The following testing information must be provided to consumers at least annually and when the drinking water supplier becomes aware that property ownership has changed: (a) a recommendation to test drinking water treated by end-point systems for E. coli and total coliforms at least every 6 months: (b) where to find a laboratory accredited by IANZ: (c) advice to increase testing to weekly for any periods when the base population temporarily increases following MUR.3(b).\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=9\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":9,\"text\":\"MUR.13 Information on how to minimise risks associated with consumers’ storage tanks must be provided The following recommendations must be provided to consumers at least annually and when the supplier becomes aware that property ownership has changed: (a) treated drinking water storage tanks should be secured against the ingress of rainwater and surface water: (b) inlets, lids, overflows and any other small gaps in treated and untreated drinking water storage tanks should be secure from contamination by vermin, birds, animals, faecal material, or other material: (c) any treated or untreated drinking water storage tanks should be inspected annually to ensure the above are met: (d) untreated water from any other source should be first delivered into an untreated water storage tank so that all water to be used for drinking passes through the end-point treatment system.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=9\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":9,\"text\":\"MUR.14 Information must be provided to owners of premises who are required to install, maintain and test end-point treatment devices If the drinking water supplier requires the owner of the premises to install, maintain, and test an end-point treatment device under section 28(3)(b) of the Act, information must be provided in writing at least annually, and when the supplier becomes aware that property ownership has changed, that advises the owner of— (a) the minimum requirements for the end-point treatment device that must be installed to meet this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution; and (b) the maintenance requirements for each end-point treatment device (for example changing UV lamps and cartridge filters); and (c) testing or monitoring requirements to verify that the end-point treatment device is operating correctly. 9\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=9\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":10,\"text\":\"Backflow prevention MUR.15 All connections must have backflow prevention (a) Backflow prevention must be installed on all connections to the mixed- use rural water scheme regardless of whether the water is used for drinking after that connection. (b) Backflow prevention must be one of the following types: (i) air gaps: (ii) non-testable dual check valves: (iii) non-testable double check valves: (iv) testable backflow prevention devices. (c) Air gaps used for backflow prevention must be inspected annually. (d) Testable backflow prevention devices must— (i) be installed if there is a medium or high backflow risk; and (ii) be inspected and tested annually.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=10\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":10,\"text\":\"Emergency management, record-keeping and capability MUR.16 Events and emergencies must be identified and managed appropriately (a) Events and emergencies that could compromise the ability of end-point treatment systems to make drinking water safe or put the supply of a sufficient quantity of drinking water at risk must be identified and documented. (b) A response plan must be developed and followed for each event or emergency identified under MUR.16(a).\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=10\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":10,\"text\":\"MUR.17 Records of the drinking water supply must be kept The following records must be kept and maintained for at least three years: (a) records of installation, maintenance and testing of end-point treatment devices: (b) records of inspection and maintenance of supply components including storage tanks, pipes and pumps: (c) records of all monitoring undertaken by the drinking water supplier: (d) records of all information provided to consumers: (e) records of installation, inspection and testing of backflow prevention: (f) records of response plans and responses to events and emergencies. MUR.18 People working on a drinking water supply must be competent People who install, maintain or operate any aspect of the drinking water supply must be suitably qualified, trained or experienced to undertake the relevant task. 10\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=10\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":11,\"text\":\"Made at Wellington on 3 September 2025. Allan Prangnell Chief Executive Water Services Authority—Taumata Arowai Explanatory note This note is not part of the drinking water acceptable solution but is intended to indicate its general effect. This drinking water acceptable solution, which comes into force on 5 September 2025, provides a prescribed set of requirements that drinking water suppliers for mixed-use rural water schemes can use to establish compliance with particular duties in the Act. End-point treatment systems consisting of cartridge filtration and UV disinfection are required at consumers’ properties as an alternative to treating drinking water centrally. An end-point treatment system can serve one building with no more than 500 people or up to 3 buildings with no more than 100 people.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=11\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":11,\"text\":\"Validated UV systems are required for buildings with over 25 people or buildings that have a community purpose or are open to the public. Drinking water supplied must be of a quality suitable for end-point treatment, key determinands must be monitored, spring and bore sources must be protected, information must be provided to consumers, backflow prevention must be installed, emergencies must be managed, records must be kept and people working on the supply must be competent. A drinking water supplier who meets the criteria and requirements is not required to comply with compliance rules made under the Act and prepare a drinking water safety plan. The Drinking Water Acceptable Solution for Mixed-use Rural Water Supplies 2022 is revoked and replaced. This is secondary legislation issued under the authority of the Legislation Act 2019.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=11\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-use Rural Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":11,\"text\":\"Title Water Services (Mixed-use Rural) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025 Principal or amendment Principal Consolidated version No Empowering Act and provisions Water Services Act 2021 Section 50 Replacement empowering Act and provisions Maker name Water Services Authority—Taumata Arowai Administering agency Water Services Authority—Taumata Arowai Date made 3 September 2025 Publication date 4 September 2025 Notification date 4 September 2025 Commencement date 5 September 2025 End date (when applicable) Consolidation as at date Not applicable Related instruments 11\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Mixed-use-Rural-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=11\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":1,\"text\":\"This legislation is administered by the Water Services Authority—Taumata Arowai. For more information please see: Website: http://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz Contact phone: +64 4 889 8350 Contact address: PO Box 628, Wellington 6140, New Zealand Water Services (Self-supplied Buildings) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025 This Drinking Water Acceptable Solution is made under section 50 of the Water Services Act 2021 by the Chief Executive of the Water Services Authority—Taumata Arowai acting under delegated authority after consultation in accordance with section 53 of that Act.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=1\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":1,\"text\":\"Contents Page 1 Title 1 2 Commencement 1 3 Interpretation 2 4 Meaning of self-supplied building drinking water supply 2 5 Application 3 6 Compliance with the Water Services Act 2021 3 7 Transition 3 Schedule 1 4 Requirements for Self-supplied Buildings 1 Title This is the Water Services (Self-supplied Buildings) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025. 2 Commencement This Drinking Water Acceptable Solution comes into force on 5 September 2025.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=1\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":2,\"text\":\"Water Services (Self-Supplied Buildings) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025 3 Interpretation In this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution, unless the context otherwise requires,— Act means the Water Services Act 2021 base population means the number of people that are normally supplied drinking water regardless of any seasonal or temporary increases bore means a piped or encased hole constructed to access groundwater building means a stand-alone structure where drinking water is provided.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=2\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":2,\"text\":\"A building does not include a structure where only non-potable water is provided for other uses. designated validation standard means one of the following: (a) NSF/ANSI 55 Class A: (b) USEPA Ultraviolet Disinfection Guidance Manual: (c) DVGW Technical Standard W294-1:2023-12: (d) ÖNORM M 5873-1: 2020 01 01: (e) DIN 19294-1:2020-08 determinand means a substance or characteristic that is determined or estimated in drinking water IANZ means International Accreditation New Zealand, a trading entity of the Accreditation Council continued under the Standards and Accreditation Act 2015 MAV means the maximum acceptable value of a determinand as set out in the Water Services (Drinking Water Standards for New Zealand) Regulations 2022 roof water means the rainwater collected from the roof of a building or structure self-supplied building drinking water supply has the meaning set out in clause 4 of this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution spring means a location where groundwater naturally emerges from the ground surface surface water means a lake, river, stream or similar body of water that is located on top of land treatment system means the combined system of treatment devices UV means ultraviolet light UV transmittance means the measurement of the amount of UV light that can pass through water usually measured as a percentage 4 Meaning of self-supplied building drinking water supply (1) In this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution, self-supplied building drinking water supply means a drinking water supply that provides drinking water to up to 10 buildings on one site within the boundaries of one property, or within the boundaries of two or more adjoining properties with common ownership arrangements.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=2\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":2,\"text\":\"(2) A self-supplied building drinking water supply must serve no more than 500 people (additional populations limits for multiple buildings apply). 2\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=2\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":3,\"text\":\"Water Services (Self-Supplied Buildings) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025 5 Application (1) This Drinking Water Acceptable Solution may be used only by drinking water suppliers who own or operate self-supplied building drinking water supplies. (2) Bore, spring, roof or surface water may be used as source water for the drinking water supply. (3) The source water may be on the drinking water supplier’s or another person’s property. (4) A self-supplied building drinking water supply may be supplied in whole or in part by another drinking water supply (for example, a registered water carrier or a networked supply).\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=3\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":3,\"text\":\"6 Compliance with the Water Services Act 2021 (1) Drinking water suppliers who ensure that a self-supplied building drinking water supply meets all the applicable requirements in Schedule 1 are deemed to comply with the following sections of the Act: (a) section 24 – duty to take all reasonable steps to supply aesthetically acceptable drinking water: (b) section 30 – owner must have a drinking water safety plan: (c) section 37(1) – drinking water suppliers to keep records: (d) section 43 – source water risk management plans: (e) section 49(3) – duty to comply with all applicable compliance rules.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=3\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":3,\"text\":\"(2) For the avoidance of doubt, drinking water suppliers who comply with the requirements in Schedule 1 are not required to prepare a drinking water safety plan (including a source water risk management plan) or demonstrate compliance with compliance rules (for example, the Drinking Water Quality Assurance Rules). 7 Transition (1) Drinking water suppliers that were using the Drinking Water Acceptable Solution for Spring and Bore Water Supplies 2022 or the Drinking Water Acceptable Solution for Roof Water Supplies 2022 at the date of commencement of this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution— (a) are deemed to be compliant with this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution for 1 year from that date if the previous requirements continue to be met; or (b) may transition to this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution at any time following commencement. 3\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=3\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":4,\"text\":\"Water Services (Self-Supplied Buildings) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025 Schedule 1 Requirements for Self-supplied Buildings Contents Page Treatment SSB.1 Treatment must be used 4 SSB.2 Treatment must meet population and building limits 5 SSB.3 Treatment must be effective 5 SSB.4 UV disinfection systems must be validated for designated buildings 6 General water quality SSB.5 Water must be suitable for treatment 6 SSB.6 Additional treatment may be used 7 Source-specific water quality SSB.7 Bore, spring and surface water must be monitored 7 SSB.8 Cyanobacteria/cyanotoxin risks must be identified and managed for surface water 7 SSB.9 Springs and bores must be protected 8 SSB.10 Roof water must be monitored 8 SSB.11 Risks to roof water quality must be minimised 9 Risk management SSB.12 Risks associated with storage tanks must be minimised 9 SSB.13 Treated drinking water must be monitored 9 SSB.14 Risks related to treated water pipes must be minimised for supplies serving between 3 and 10 buildings or more than 100 people 10 Emergency management, record-keeping and capability SSB.15 Events and emergencies must be managed appropriately 11 SSB.16 Records of the drinking water supply must be kept 11 SSB.17 People working on the drinking water supply must be competent 11 Treatment SSB.1 Treatment must be used Treatment that meets the requirements of SSB.2 to SSB.4 must be installed and maintained for any buildings where the water supplied is used as drinking water.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=4\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":4,\"text\":\"4\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=4\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":5,\"text\":\"Water Services (Self-Supplied Buildings) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025 SSB.2 Treatment must meet population and building limits (a) A treatment system must either— (i) provide drinking water to a single building where the base population does not exceed 500 people; or (ii) provide drinking water to no more than three buildings on one property, or on adjoining properties with common ownership arrangements, where the base population does not exceed 100 people; or (iii) provide drinking water to no more than 10 buildings on one property, or on adjoining properties with common ownership arrangements, where the base population does not exceed 500 people where the requirements of SSB.14 are met.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=5\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":5,\"text\":\"(b) The population supplied by a treatment system may exceed its base population limit if— (i) the exceedance is for a total of no more than 60 days in any 12- month period; and (ii) the treatment system has the capacity to effectively treat drinking water for these periods.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=5\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":5,\"text\":\"SSB.3 Treatment must be effective The following requirements must be met to ensure that a treatment system is effective for treating drinking water: (a) a treatment system must consist of at a least a cartridge filtration system and a UV disinfection system: (b) each component of a treatment system must be installed— (i) to meet the peak instantaneous demand for treated drinking water; and (ii) in accordance with the manufacturer’s instructions and requirements: (c) a treatment system must include at least one cartridge filter, installed before the UV disinfection system and with the final or only cartridge having a 5 micron or less nominal pore size: (d) every device in the treatment system must be operated and maintained in accordance with the manufacturer’s instructions: (e) the UV disinfection system must deliver at least 40 mJ/cm2 reduction equivalent dose of UV light: (f) water flowing through the UV disinfection system must be restricted or monitored so that the flow rate does not exceed the flow rate specified by the manufacturer for 40 mJ/cm2 reduction equivalent dose of UV light: (g) lamp usage in the UV disinfection system must not exceed manufacturer's recommendations: 5\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=5\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":6,\"text\":\"Water Services (Self-Supplied Buildings) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025 (h) written evidence must be available (for example, from the manufacturer’s website, or the instruction manual) confirming— (i) the UV disinfection system delivers at least 40 mJ/cm2 reduction equivalent dose of UV light; and (ii) the flow rate specified by the manufacturer for 40 mJ/cm2 reduction equivalent dose of UV light.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=6\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":6,\"text\":\"SSB.4 UV disinfection systems must be validated for designated buildings A UV disinfection system must be validated to and operate within the specifications of a designated validation standard for any buildings— (a) with a base population that exceeds 25 people; or (b) with a base population of any size, if— (i) the building has a community purpose (for example, a school, marae, sports club, or community hall); or (ii) the building has drinking water available to the public (for example a cafe, hotel, or camping ground); but (c) notwithstanding (a) and (b), validation is not required where a UV disinfection system was installed before 17 October 2022 and written evidence is available (for example, the manufacturer’s website, or the instruction manual) that the unit delivers at least 40 mJ/cm2 reduction equivalent dose of UV light.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=6\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":6,\"text\":\"General water quality SSB.5 Water must be suitable for treatment All water intended to be used as drinking water must meet the following requirements to be suitable for a cartridge filtration and UV disinfection treatment system: (a) turbidity must be low enough to ensure cartridge filters will last for at least one month before replacement of cartridges is required: (b) UV transmittance must be high enough for the UV disinfection system to achieve at least 40 mJ/cm2 reduction equivalent dose of UV light: (c) iron must be low enough to ensure the UV disinfection system sleeve is not fouled with iron deposits within one year before cleaning is required: (d) manganese must be low enough to ensure the UV disinfection system sleeve is not fouled with manganese deposits within one year before cleaning is required: (e) chemical determinands that are required to be monitored under SSB.7 and SSB.10 must not exceed their respective MAV in the drinking water standards: (f) where surface water is used, cyanobacteria/cyanotoxin risk must be either assessed as low or managed to mitigate the risk prior to water entering the treatment system.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=6\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":6,\"text\":\"6\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=6\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":7,\"text\":\"Water Services (Self-Supplied Buildings) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025 SSB.6 Additional treatment may be used (a) Additional treatment may be used to improve water quality to meet the requirements of SSB.5 prior to cartridge filtration and UV treatment. (b) Chlorine may be added to a drinking water supply to control bacterial and viral pathogens and to reduce the risk of growth of biofilms however, it is not required to meet this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution. Source-specific water quality SSB.7 Bore, spring and surface water must be monitored (a) Where the source water is or includes bore, spring or surface water, the following determinands must be monitored in untreated water before this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution is used and then— (i) at least every three months for the following microbiological determinands: a.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=7\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":7,\"text\":\"E. coli: b. total coliforms; and (ii) at least every three months for the following physical determinands: a. turbidity: b. UV transmittance; and (iii) at least annually for the following chemical determinands: a. iron: b. manganese: c. nitrate: d. arsenic: e. boron: f. hardness: g. any other chemical determinands that are identified as presenting a risk to the supply. (b) Microbiological and chemical samples collected for SSB.7(a) must be— (i) collected according to any instructions and specifications provided by the laboratory; and (ii) analysed by a laboratory accredited by IANZ for the type of analysis being undertaken. SSB.8 Cyanobacteria/cyanotoxin risks must be identified and managed for surface water Where the source water is or includes surface water, the following requirements must be met: 7\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=7\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":8,\"text\":\"Water Services (Self-Supplied Buildings) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025 (a) monitoring or visual inspections of the source water and area around and upstream of any surface water intake must be undertaken each month between October and May (inclusive) for the presence of benthic cyanobacteria mats and planktonic cyanobacterial growth: (b) if there is evidence of cyanobacterial growth steps must be taken to assess the cyanotoxin risk: (c) if there is a risk of supplying water with cyanotoxins that exceed a MAV, a response plan must be developed and followed under SSB.15: (d) taste or odour concerns, which have the potential to relate to cyanobacteria, must be— (i) recorded; and (ii) investigated to determine the cause.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=8\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":8,\"text\":\"SSB.9 Springs and bores must be protected Where the source water is or includes a spring or a bore, the following requirements must be met: (a) springs and bores must be protected by headworks which minimise the risk of contamination from nearby surface water: (b) farm animals must be excluded from an area extending at least five metres in all directions from the headworks of a bore, spring or spring-fed pond: (c) springs and bores must not be affected by contamination from any of the following: (i) a sewage disposal field or effluent discharge (for example, a septic tank or other wastewater treatment system): (ii) an underground storage tank (such as at a petrol station): (iii) a waste pond: (iv) a landfill: (v) an offal pit: (vi) areas where pesticides or animal effluent is applied to land: (vii) aquifers contaminated with, or at risk of contamination with, sewage from exfiltration and/or pump station overflows: (viii) contaminated sites: (d) springs and bores must not provide geothermal water.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=8\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":8,\"text\":\"SSB.10 Roof water must be monitored (a) Where the source water is or includes roof water, the following determinands must be monitored in untreated roof water before this Drinking Water Acceptable Solution is used and then— (i) at least every three months for the following microbiological determinands: a. E. coli: b. total coliforms; and 8\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=8\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":9,\"text\":\"Water Services (Self-Supplied Buildings) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025 (ii) at least every three years for the following chemical determinands: a. cadmium: b. copper: c. lead. (b) Microbiological and chemical samples collected for SSB.10(a) must be— (i) collected according to any instructions and specifications provided by the laboratory; and (ii) analysed by a laboratory accredited by IANZ for the type of analysis being undertaken.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=9\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":9,\"text\":\"SSB.11 Risks to roof water quality must be minimised Where the source water is or includes roof water, the following requirements must be met: (a) as far as practicable, roof surfaces used for the collection of drinking water must be kept free from decaying debris, leaves, branches, bird nests and faeces from animals or birds: (b) the roof supply must be able to be isolated from the water storage and treatment system for cleaning and maintenance.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=9\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":9,\"text\":\"Risk management SSB.12 Risks associated with storage tanks must be minimised (a) Treated water storage tanks must be inspected at least every three months to ensure— (i) tanks are secured against the accidental ingress of rainwater and surface water; and (ii) inlets, lids, overflows and any other small gaps in tanks are secure from contamination by vermin, birds, animals, faecal material, or other material. (b) Untreated water storage tanks must be inspected at least annually to ensure they are secured from contamination by vermin, birds, animals, faecal material, or other material. (c) Untreated water from any source additional to the primary source must be first delivered into an untreated water storage tank so that all water to be used for drinking passes through the treatment system.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=9\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":9,\"text\":\"SSB.13 Treated drinking water must be monitored (a) The following determinands must be monitored in treated drinking water: (i) E. coli: (ii) total coliforms. (b) Samples collected for SSB.13(a) must be— 9\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=9\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":10,\"text\":\"Water Services (Self-Supplied Buildings) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025 (i) collected from within the building where the treatment system is located, or if more than one building is supplied then from a tap in a building that is the greatest distance from the building where the treatment system is located; and (ii) collected according to any instructions and specifications provided by the laboratory; and (iii) analysed by a laboratory accredited by IANZ for the type of analysis being undertaken. (c) Samples collected for SSB.13(a) must be collected at the following intervals: (i) every three months if the population is 100 or less: (ii) monthly if the population is 101 or more.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=10\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":10,\"text\":\"(d) Where the base population is 100 or less, and the population temporarily increases above 100 but below 500, additional samples of treated water must be collected and tested for microbiological determinands— (i) in the week before the population increases if the increase is planned; and (ii) weekly until the population reduces below 100. (e) Where the population temporarily increases above 500, additional samples for microbiological determinands must be collected— (i) in the week before the population increases if the increase is planned; and (ii) twice weekly until the population reduces below 500.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=10\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":10,\"text\":\"SSB.14 Risks related to treated water pipes must be minimised for supplies serving between 3 and 10 buildings or more than 100 people (a) SSB.14(b) applies if— (i) drinking water is provided to more than three buildings from the same treatment system; or (ii) the drinking water supply serves more than 100 people and is provided to more than one building from the same treatment system. (b) Pipes that carry treated drinking water to buildings must not be laid close to or through any of the following: (i) a sewage disposal field or effluent discharge (for example, a septic tank or other wastewater treatment system): (ii) an underground storage tank (such as at a petrol station): (iii) a waste pond: (iv) a landfill: (v) an offal pit: (vi) areas where pesticides or animal effluent is applied to land: (vii) contaminated sites. 10\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=10\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":11,\"text\":\"Water Services (Self-Supplied Buildings) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025 Emergency management, record-keeping and capability SSB.15 Events and emergencies must be managed appropriately (a) Events and emergencies that could put the safety of drinking water or the supply of a sufficient quantity of drinking water at risk must be identified and documented. (b) A response plan must be developed and followed for each event or emergency identified under SSB.15(a).\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=11\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":11,\"text\":\"SSB.16 Records of the drinking water supply must be kept The following records must be kept and maintained for at least three years: (a) records of installation and maintenance of treatment devices: (b) records of inspection and maintenance of supply components including storage tanks, pipes and pumps: (c) records of all monitoring: (d) records of response plans and responses to events and emergencies. SSB.17 People working on the drinking water supply must be competent People who install, maintain or operate any aspect of the drinking water supply must be suitably qualified, trained or experienced to undertake the relevant task. Made at Wellington on 3 September 2025. Allan Prangnell Chief Executive Water Services Authority—Taumata Arowai 11\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=11\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":12,\"text\":\"Water Services (Self-Supplied Buildings) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025 Explanatory note This note is not part of the Drinking Water Acceptable Solution but is intended to indicate its general effect. This drinking water acceptable solution, which comes into force on 5 September 2025, provides a prescribed set of requirements that self-supplied building drinking water suppliers can use to establish compliance with particular duties in the Act. Self-supplied building is defined as the supply of drinking water to up to 10 buildings on one site within the boundaries of one property or multiple properties with common ownership arrangements that serves no more than 500 people.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=12\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":12,\"text\":\"Additional population limits are 500 for a single building, 100 for up to three buildings or 500 for up to 10 buildings where additional risk management requirements are met. A treatment system consisting of cartridge filtration and UV disinfection must be installed. Validated UV systems are required for buildings with over 25 people or buildings that have a community purpose or are open to the public. Water must be of a quality suitable for the treatment system, key determinands must be monitored, spring, bore and roof sources must be protected, risks to tanks and pipes must be managed, emergencies must be managed, records must be kept and people working on the supply must be competent.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=12\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":12,\"text\":\"A drinking water supplier who meets all the applicable requirements is not required to comply with compliance rules made under the Act and prepare a drinking water safety plan. The Drinking Water Acceptable Solution for Spring and Bore Water Supplies 2022 and the Drinking Water Acceptable Solution for Roof Water Supplies 2022 have been replaced with separate acceptable solutions for small to medium networks and self-supplied buildings. This is secondary legislation issued under the authority of the Legislation Act 2019.\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=12\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"},{\"title\":\"Self-supplied Buildings Acceptable Solution 2025\",\"page\":12,\"text\":\"Title Water Services (Self-supplies Buildings) Drinking Water Acceptable Solution 2025 Principal or amendment Principal Consolidated version No Empowering Act and provisions Water Services Act 2021 Section 50 Replacement empowering Act and provisions Maker name Water Services Authority—Taumata Arowai Administering agency Water Services Authority—Taumata Arowai Date made 3 September 2025 Publication date 4 September 2025 Notification date 4 September 2025 Commencement date 5 September 2025 End date (when applicable) Consolidation as at date Not applicable Related instruments 12\",\"url\":\"https://www.taumataarowai.govt.nz/assets/Uploads/Acceptable-Solutions-etc/Water-Services-Self-supplied-Buildings-Drinking-Water-Acceptable-Solution-2025.pdf#page=12\",\"kind\":\"acceptable\"}]");
